iction (`ast_walker.cljc:401`, `:402-404`); the linearizer admits any |\\\\n288:| :application            | 4          | [:application operator operands      | operator operands tail?  | node, nodes, bool| :371-376           | :424-429        |\\\\n330:**`tail?` is a slot of `:application` only.** The one reader of the tail\\\\n332:(`linearize.cljc:107-108`), which reads it on `:application`; the walker\\\\n339:mark on an `:if`'s branches is carried by the branch `:application` nodes\\\\n361:| `:application` tail?                               | `false`                             | codec emits only `true` (`v2.cljc:412`); the linearizer reads `(boolean …)` |\\\\n364:| `:application` / `:dao.stream.apply/call` operands | `[]`                                | walker `ast_walker.cljc:383`                                                |\\\\n367:### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\\\\n384:  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\\\\n405:- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\\\\n411:  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\\\\n426:An **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\\\\n430:is keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\\\\n436:removes. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\\\\n453:| harvest catalogue      | `[[:source medium batch] h j path]`: occurrence `[j path]` belongs to the `h`-th **original definition** in the        | the frontend or the  |\\\\n512:  (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`), while an application interprets an\\\\n519:  effect descriptor under the applicable execution contract**; a result\\\\n520:  the contract would interpret is stored by the arm and executed by the\\\\n568:| lowering as an address-to-address function    | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and each application of it is one derivation record (§5.2)                        |\\\\n596:contract precisely and its remaining residuals: scalar (symbol) metadata\\\\n636:### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\\\\n645:`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\\\\n659:\\\\\\\"This call site\\\\\\\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\\\\n661:joins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\\\\n663:or a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\\\\n665:occurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\\\\n683:occurrence side-table) and check whether the `:variable`'s `name` appears\\\\n696:cosmetic; the no-fallthrough contract (`:variable`'s lexical-only\\\\n715:`:application`'s operator/operands slots), not generic over §2.3's full\\\\n725:at one occurrence and bound at another — and this is not a permanent\\\\n728:free at one occurrence and bound at another (e.g. `((fn [x] x) x)`, whose\\\\n733:which would silently drop the genuinely free occurrence from a computed\\\\n741:*specific occurrence* is exactly a places-not-contents question, and\\\\n742:§6.1 already routes those through the occurrence relation\\\\n744:each occurrence's own path to its ancestors, not the row's abstracted\\\\n746:`occurrence-aware-rules-resolve-mixed-free-and-bound-rows` proves it\\\\n748:occurrence relation of §2.5's shape as `yin.vm/occurrences` emits it, a\\\\n754:richer, occurrence-joined query, exactly as this section claims.\\\\n756:**Root-scoped, and portable data.** A production occurrence relation\\\\n757:holds every reachable tree's occurrences together, and structural paths\\\\n761:misclassify an occurrence in one tree against a binder in another;\\\\n764:`yin.vm/occurrence-rules`, is one non-recursive rule over explicitly\\\\n778:A name is bound at `[root path]` iff a `:lambda` occurrence **of the same\\\\n790:`occurrence-walk-covers-the-whole-grammar` resolves binding through `:if`\\\\n792:emits the occurrence tuples is `yin.vm/occurrences`.\\\\n802:slot of a two-operand application whose first operand is a literal\\\\n803:symbol other than `yin/def`; every other occurrence, a `:lambda` binding\\\\n826:strictly retain named variables. Therefore, every contract name in this\\\\n836:tree-address ──lower under lowering profile L, targeting execution contract C──▶ segment-address\\\\n850:                       :yin.code/contract \\\\\\\"v3\\\\\\\"  ; UCF S7.3.3 contract\\\\n861:UCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\\\\n864:two deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\\\\n873:| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\\\\n882:| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\\\\n886:contract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\\\\n888:execution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\\\\n907:   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_11\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'J2|portable sources|call.site' docs/design/yin.repl.md docs/design/yang.antlr.md docs/design/yin.vm.jolt.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_11\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'J2|portable sources|call.site' docs/design/yin.repl.md docs/design/yang.antlr.md docs/design/yin.vm.jolt.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"rg: docs/design/yin.repl.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\nrg: docs/design/yin.vm.jolt.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:3529:UAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:3699:PHP call sites include it when their declared target has reference\\\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:3746:may remain as source call-site helpers, but must not reject an admitted\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":2,\\\"status\\\":\\\"failed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_12\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '115,148p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '367,445p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '636,669p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; rg --files docs/design | rg 'jolt|repl|clojure'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_12\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '115,148p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '367,445p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '636,669p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; rg --files docs/design | rg 'jolt|repl|clojure'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"(traces, stream facts, stigmergy, schema, data) is datoms; this design only\\\\npulled code out of them and made it properly immutable.\\\\n\\\\nSaid plainly: the datom layer is not a description of the code; it is the\\\\nonly place the code's name, past, and ownership exist. Content says what\\\\nthe program is; datoms say which program we mean, where it came from, and\\\\nwho may run it.\\\\n\\\\nWhat never crosses:\\\\n\\\\n- **Nothing about time, naming, occurrence, or provenance enters code\\\\n  content.** `t`, `m`, entity ids, source positions, the macro flag, the\\\\n  expansion that produced a tree, the AST a segment came from, and the\\\\n  address itself are all outside the hashed value. UCF §7.3.2 already\\\\n  excludes these from the instruction vector; §2.5 applies the same\\\\n  exclusion to the AST. The consequence is stated, not hidden: code\\\\n  content does not know its predecessor, so the chain between values is\\\\n  held by ledger records, never by the values themselves (§8.2).\\\\n- **No code content enters a ref.** A ref carries an address and nothing\\\\n  else about the code; two ledgers that agree on `dao.jing` addresses\\\\n  interoperate without exchanging trees.\\\\n- **No datom-projection tempid is ever named by a ref, a ledger row, a\\\\n  closure, or a continuation.** The datom projection's `e` values are\\\\n  local; only addresses cross layers, and this is what retires the\\\\n  `:eid` sharing of `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:394-411` (§4.4).\\\\n- **Content identity is not occurrence identity.** An address says *what*\\\\n  a value is. Which source position, which batch, which call site, which\\\\n  expansion attempt produced it is occurrence information, and it is kept\\\\n  in side tables and events keyed by an origin (source batch or producing\\\\n  event), root address, and structural path (§2.5, §4.4, §8.4), never by\\\\n  content address alone.\\\\n- **The hot loop reads content only.** An evaluator builds its image at\\\\n  load — the walker reconstructs the map AST from rows, the semantic VM\\\\n  decodes the vector — and never consults a ref or a projection while\\\\n### 2.5 Exclusions, occurrences, and side tables\\\\n\\\\nExclusion is a rule of the projection boundary, the same rule §2.4 states\\\\nfor saturation: these facts are stripped map→rows and never re-derived\\\\nrows→map. The map AST a frontend emits carries them; no row does:\\\\n\\\\n- **Source positions** and any frontend metadata (`:yang/*` keys, Clojure\\\\n  reader metadata on symbols). Tracked instead in secondary side tables.\\\\n  **Parameter and variable names are not an exclusion**: the boundary\\\\n  projection retains them in the canonical tuples (`:lambda params`,\\\\n  `:variable name`), since this design preserves named variables across\\\\n  the boundary.\\\\n- **`:macro?`** and `:phase-policy`. `yang.clojure` sets them on defmacro\\\\n  lambdas (`clojure.cljc:436-438`) and the codec persists `:yin/macro?`\\\\n  (`v2.cljc:419-420`, schema `v2.cljc:312`). No evaluator reads them\\\\n  (`ast_walker.cljc:20-23`). The expander needs the fact that a particular\\\\n  definition is a macro; that fact travels beside the tree as an\\\\n  occurrence-bound declaration (§8.5), not as a slot.\\\\n- **`:eid`** (§4.4).\\\\n- **`:yin/root`.** A tree is its own root; the batch or the ref that names\\\\n  the tree names the root. No root fact exists in a row — the root row's\\\\n  id is the root fact (§4.1).\\\\n- **`t`, `m`, provenance.** Every provenance fact the codec or linearizer\\\\n  writes today (`:yin.code/source`, `:yin.code/derived-from`) becomes a\\\\n  ledger record (§8) or a side-table entry.\\\\n\\\\n**Occurrences.** Content identity names *what*; three further things\\\\nname *where*, and each is a distinct coordinate:\\\\n\\\\n- A **structural path** names a place inside one tree: a vector of slot\\\\n  indices from the root, with an index into a `nodes` slot written as a\\\\n  pair `[slot i]`. `[]` is the root; `[2 [3 0]]` is the first operand of\\\\n  the root's second slot. Slot indices are the row's positions, which the\\\\n  §2.3 dictionary fixes against the map's key order, so a path reads\\\\n  identically on both sides of the round trip. A path is a pure function\\\\n  of the tree, stable across media and hosts. `[root-address path]`\\\\n  separates two identical subtrees at two places in one tree, and nothing\\\\n  more.\\\\n- A **source occurrence** names one admission of one tree: `[medium batch\\\\n  j]`, the program medium's identity, the batch coordinate on it (the\\\\n  `:yin/source-batch` of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1), and the **member index**\\\\n  `j` of the tree within the batch (§8.5's ordered batch carries several\\\\n  trees). Two identical trees parsed from two files, admitted in two\\\\n  batches, or sitting at two indices of one batch have one root address\\\\n  and two source occurrences. A root address alone never identifies a\\\\n  source, and neither does a batch without its member index.\\\\n  Here `medium` is the program medium's `:dao.stream/identity` and `batch`\\\\n  is the admitting composition's fresh plain-data token, carried as\\\\n  `:yin/source-medium` and `:yin/batch-token` in the batch envelope. A retry\\\\n  of an unaccepted staged append keeps that token; a distinct admission\\\\n  mints another. Neither coordinate is inferred from cursor internals or\\\\n  content.\\\\n- A **producing event** names one generated tree: the `:expand` event\\\\n  (§8.4) whose output it is. Two expansion attempts with identical output\\\\n  have one output address and two producing events. An event has two\\\\n  identities, used in two places: a **log-local entity id**, which only a\\\\n  declared reference attribute on the same log may name, and the\\\\n  **address of its expansion record** (§8.2), which is portable.\\\\n\\\\nAn **occurrence key** is therefore `[origin root-address path]`, where\\\\n`origin` is `[:source medium batch j]` for admitted syntax and\\\\n`[:expansion record-address]` for generated syntax. Every side table and\\\\nevery provenance link that must distinguish places, sources, or attempts\\\\nis keyed by an occurrence key, never by an address alone, and the member\\\\nindex travels in the origin uniformly: declarations, source positions,\\\\ninstruction provenance, and initial expansion events all carry the same\\\\n`[:source medium batch j]`. Joining a position table to a batch table on\\\\nroot address is not a substitute: it yields every batch-position\\\\ncombination, which is exactly the ambiguity the origin coordinate\\\\nremoves. A bare log-local event number never appears inside an occurrence\\\\nkey, because a key travels and the number does not (§8.4).\\\\n\\\\n+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\\\\n| Side table             | Row                                                                                                                    | Keeper               |\\\\n+========================+========================================================================================================================+======================+\\\\n| source positions       | `[[:source medium batch j] root-address path file line col]`                                                           | the frontend that    |\\\\n|                        |                                                                                                                        | parsed the file      |\\\\n+------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------+\\\\n| frontend metadata      | `[origin root-address path key value]`                                                                                 | the frontend         |\\\\n### 4.4 Sharing by content address replaces `:eid`; occurrences stay outside\\\\n\\\\nToday a shared subtree is emitted once and referenced by a pre-assigned\\\\nentity id: `yang.clojure` allocates one for each defmacro lambda\\\\n(`clojure.cljc:418-437`) and the codec deduplicates on it\\\\n(`v2.cljc:394-411`). Its purpose is that the definition operand and each\\\\ncall-site operator resolve to one entity so provenance can point at it.\\\\n\\\\nContent addressing gives that for free and in every case, not only the one\\\\n`yang.clojure` remembers to mark: two occurrences of one subtree have one\\\\naddress by construction and one row in the stored set, and a query \\\\\\\"which\\\\nsites reference this lambda's content\\\\\\\" is a join on its address (§6.4).\\\\nBecause this design retains named variables in the canonical tuple (§2, §5.1),\\\\nthis structural sharing requires exact identity, not alpha-equivalence:\\\\ntwo functions that are identical up to variable renaming project to\\\\n*different* rows, since the parameter and variable names are part of the\\\\nhashed content. Only subtrees identical in structure *and* in every retained\\\\nname collapse to one row. The row set holds each such distinct subtree once,\\\\nand parent slots point at it — while identity stays per row.\\\\n**This structural collapse depends on the §4.2 encoder being injective, which it now is for ordinary values under default print bindings.** The three conformance pairs (vector/list, vector/seq, collection metadata) that used to make the encoder non-injective are closed; structurally distinct ordinary values no longer hash equal under the default host print configuration. The disclosed residuals remain gaps: scalar metadata and byte-array identity hashing need a future producer to matter here, but ambient print-var bindings reaching scalar bytes are live now — a row whose slots include scalars hashed inside such a binding is not covered by this collapse guarantee — see §4.2.\\\\n`:eid` is dropped from the frontends and the codec.\\\\n\\\\nWhat `:eid` also did, and content addresses do not, is name a *place*.\\\\n\\\\\\\"This call site\\\\\\\" is an occurrence key `[origin root-address path]` (§2.5),\\\\nnot the site's content address; a query that needs distinct call sites\\\\njoins the occurrence relation, not subtree addresses, and a provenance\\\\nlink that must survive a second identical site, a second identical file,\\\\nor a second identical expansion records the occurrence key. Content\\\\naddresses serve sharing and cross-media correspondence of content;\\\\noccurrence keys serve identity of places, sources, and attempts.\\\\n\\\\n### 4.5 Free variables are queried, not tagged\\\\n\\\\nAn earlier revision of this design split variable references into two\\\\ndocs/design/yin.repl.link-policy.md\\\\ndocs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md\\\\ndocs/design/yin-repl-design.md\\\\ndocs/design/yin.repl.dao.space-index.md\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_13\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"I found a contract conflict for Astra to resolve: the code-as-tuples design already defines call-site identity as an occurrence key and keeps provenance outside hashed AST content. The addendum needs to distinguish annotating an application occurrence from adding a canonical application-node field.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_14\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n '\\\\\\\"'^##|''^###|caller|site|UAST|schema|version'\\\\\\\"' collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_14\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n '\\\\\\\"'^##|''^###|caller|site|UAST|schema|version'\\\\\\\"' collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"5:# Task: implement the SIGNED attribution-as-query ruling — caller-side site tagging, derived site tables, the seven-case battery, the honest migration\\\\n9:## Context\\\\n11:Worktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-aaq, branch attribution-as-query, base master 3eebe844e (the ruling landed; D2, cell/authentic?, H3-2, H3-3 all in). The governing reviews (astra/qwen/kimi) are linked from the ruling's header — kimi's v3 review found the mechanism flaw your implementation must respect: **site ids are CALLER-SIDE, at application expressions in the compiled AST, post-macroexpansion; the callee-entry record carries the CALLER's site id.** Callee-side tagging (v3's flaw) yields identical records for both origins and is forbidden.\\\\n13:## The implementation, in the ruling's stated order\\\\n15:1. **FIRST DELIVERABLE — the written inventory confirmation**: what the instrumentation change touches (kernel-trace-form's position, the walker's application-node stamping site, the callee-record extension), the capacity measurement (stream/make + the instruction bound vs the added trace field — R6), and the offset-table coverage list (every context-API arity kernel-context-extra-arguments served, latte_oracle_test.clj:1786). Write it BEFORE code; commit it as the round's first commit.\\\\n16:2. **Caller-side site-id stamping**: application expressions in the compiled AST (post-macroexpansion — the corpus runs under is/testing/LaTTe macros; source-level tagging misses expanded sites) get compile-time-unique site ids; the callee-entry record becomes `[fname arity caller-site-id]`. The walker changes are AUTHORIZED by the ruling (R2) — scoped to application-node stamping + the record extension; nothing else in yin.vm moves.\\\\n17:3. **Mechanically derived site tables**: the compiler derives each site's argument shape (ambient literal `empty-env` vs anything else) during instrumentation — the tables are DERIVED, not hand-declared (R7's trust model), recorded in a stable versioned evidence format (R1's condition b), with the canonical-source-hash per fixture (R2b case 3's referent).\\\\n23:## Verification (test-boundary ruling: focused local, suite-scale remote)\\\\n29:## Boundaries\\\\n31:- yin.vm: ONLY the application-node stamping + the callee-record extension (R2's authorization). No other VM changes. yang.clojure: untouched unless the stamping requires it (justify). The harness (latte_oracle_test.clj, context.edn, slice9_gate.md's versioned attribution section) is the main surface. The pin migrations are authorized by R5 — list every revised pin in the evidence.\\\\n34:## Report\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_15\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'J2|portable|budget|contract|rebuild' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md | head -65; sed -n '3510,3545p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'application|occurrence' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast.cljc src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc | head -30\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_15\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n 'J2|portable|budget|contract|rebuild' docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md | head -65; sed -n '3510,3545p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'application|occurrence' src/cljc/yin/vm/ast.cljc src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc | head -30\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"5:VM, not a source-to-jolt compiler and not a new dao.stream contract. The\\\\n7:contracts and conformance oracle stay shared. jolt branches are added only where\\\\n19:walks (`f6e29391`); J2 test lane, CBOR host seam and libcrypto random bytes\\\\n21:not merged: the UDP probe stays on `jolt-j0b` (`6fa97400`). J2 is closed\\\\n22:(2026-10-24, master `f4d6a284`): J2 rerun 3 on shiva at `078e1e43` ran all 150\\\\n36:host-boundary seat's independent focused run (16/217/0). After J2 (2026-10-24):\\\\n40:deftype now throws, as on the JVM; 156 included / 53 excluded); J2b is closed\\\\n44:and the JavaScript/Dart lexer-helper manifest patches. J2b closing is necessary\\\\n72:namespace requires `dao.stream.transit`; the portable domain moves to a\\\\n121:  (\\\\\\\"implementations do not add contracts\\\\\\\").\\\\n124:  the landed datagram contract.\\\\n128:  contract and the K0/W0 fixtures (§10.4).\\\\n169:- No performance budget. Benchmarks are reported from J0 on; thresholds are\\\\n273:  surface and contract tests.\\\\n294:guard before `:clj`. The portable `dao.stream.ws` and `dao.stream.datagram` do\\\\n310:JS/Dart roots. `:replace-deps` holds the portable source dependencies this lane\\\\n319:and test aliases, bootstrap/native tasks and J2 lane (`0d07e55d`, `a0a49b8e`).\\\\n363:verified starting set, not a claim that the 91-namespace closure is portable.\\\\n461:| UDP                            | Narrow nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM adapter under the landed datagram contract (§8).                                          | J0b, J7     |\\\\n463:| WebSocket                      | Native RFC 6455 host adapter supplying the full host contract, CBOR subprotocol only (§9).                              | J6          |\\\\n488:  apply the one-bit truth table, rebuild with exact powers; a result with bit\\\\n524:overlay `boring.cljc:50`) is a wire-writer node-contract mismatch: the `:clj`\\\\n561:landed in J2; this does not close J4's file or authority integration gates.\\\\n566:  same input array**, which is returned. Keep the comparator's sign contract and\\\\n652:portable C implementation; SIMD is a separately tested optimization.\\\\n655:(`test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc`,\\\\n720:gate. No numeric JVM-ratio budget exists before J8 evidence.\\\\n836:`yang/python/antlr/repl.cljc:26-32` is portable lowering. Removing one catalog\\\\n956:nesting/resource budget for that exact thread and build across bracket,\\\\n1019:  destroyed on the caller's stack. The §7.5 contract is unchanged: jolt makes\\\\n1073:sourced Python (J6), slice J5c adds a deterministic work budget (prediction\\\\n1081:A refusal is operational: `{:diagnostic :parser-budget :axis :input|:nesting\\\\n1091:Platforms. J5b's budget, crash, calibration and time legs ran on macOS arm64\\\\n1095:On Linux x86_64 (shiva, 2026-10-29) the budget and below legs ran green at the\\\\n1100:prediction reach set, which leaks once per parser-budget refusal because the\\\\n1135:`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`; the owner split these into J2b on 2026-10-22\\\\n1195:### 8.1 The landed datagram contract (owner Q7)\\\\n1197:Owner: \\\\\\\"keep landed contract\\\\\\\". V: `src/cljc/dao/stream/datagram.cljc:25-33`\\\\n1267:datagram-contract and DHT/linker composition are the main effort:\\\\n1283:### 9.2 The host contract (W1)\\\\n1288:contract, not only the per-connection map:\\\\n1346:- the full host contract of §9.2, with per-connection ownership and no callback\\\\n1398:  reason \\\\\\\"Transit codec, owner-ruled out on jolt\\\\\\\" in the J2 applicability\\\\n1510:landings and J0c's probe do not discharge J2b or J5 acceptance.\\\\n1536:| probe-only; landed                          | and helpers,      | restricted lowering corpus, two-worker/cache isolation, collect-safe calls, thread/stack budget,    |\\\\n1554:| J2: pure test lane and VM conformance       | `test:jolt`       | Every applicable pure test loads and passes; semantic and register boundary traces match. No silent |\\\\n1565:| J2b: deferred comparisons                   | Oracle/lowering   | Node/Dart oracle comparison and restricted lowering comparison (§7.8); both close before J5. Owner  |\\\\n1566:| closed (zero pins, JVM canonical)           |                   | split from J2, 2026-10-22 (§13.5); JVM canonical, divergent hosts patched (owner 2026-10-24).       |\\\\n1574:|                                             | catalog, shell    | selectable since J5 (the native C++ parser, §7.6 budget).                                           |\\\\n1582:| J4 closed 2026-10-28;                       |                   | never overwritten; no J4-owned entry remains in jolt-applicability.edn or the J2 lane; cross-host   |\\\\n1590:|                                             | ness-probe        | Amended (row 59):