in-owned stages. Each has\\\\nexplicit inputs, outputs, and dependencies. None requires Yang core to\\\\nrecognize its language by name.\\\\n\\\\n### 8.10 Shared function calling convention\\\\n\\\\n**Status: proposed, 2026-10-29; not implemented.** This section specifies\\\\none invocation convention for every conforming ANTLR-based Yang frontend.\\\\nExamples are non-exhaustive. Adding a language must not add a case to Yang\\\\ncore, a language-pair bridge, or an evaluator transition. The words\\\\n**must**, **must not**, and **may** state requirements on a future\\\\nconforming implementation, not claims about current code.\\\\n\\\\n#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\\\\n\\\\nA Yin function is a Yin function, independent of its source syntax. The\\\\nVM applies a closure to operands. Source calling semantics are ordinary\\\\nUAST implementing the call site and callee entry, above the VM.\\\\n\\\\nEvery supported source-level callable, after its source-required\\\\nresolution, exposes `:yang.call/entry`, an ordinary Yin closure taking\\\\nexactly one invocation record. Invoking it is an ordinary\\\\n`:application`. No AST tag, lambda slot, instruction, host primitive,\\\\neffect kind, or source-language test is added to the VM.\\\\n\\\\nThis is an internal calling convention, not a transport, another system\\\\nABI, or a host callback interface. Calls between source languages inside\\\\none task do not themselves cross a host boundary. Portable I/O and\\\\ncross-task interaction continue through `dao.stream`.\\\\n\\\\n**Transparent calling** means that an admitted callable can be passed,\\\\nstored, returned, and invoked by any conforming frontend without a\\\\nhandwritten language-pair wrapper. It does not mean every foreign value\\\\nsatisfies every source operation. Invocation, name visibility, and value\\\\nrg: src/cljc/yin/vm/ast.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:4:   `:application`, `:if`, `:vm/current-continuation`); nothing here adds a\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:17:     (yin/def k v)     -> the definition application\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:18:     (f a ...)         -> :application\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc:41:  {:type :application, :operator op, :operands (vec args)})\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_16\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_16\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_17\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Astra has confirmed that the implementation already has occurrence side tables and instruction provenance mappings. The remaining checks are rollout coverage—including production Clojure—and the compatibility and budget evidence required before adoption.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_18\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '845,910p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '1900,1940p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_18\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '845,910p' docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md; sed -n '1900,1940p' docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"{:yin.ledger/op       :derive\\\\n :yin.ledger/input    <tree-address>\\\\n :yin.ledger/output   <segment-address>\\\\n :yin.ledger/function :yin.vm/lower\\\\n :yin.ledger/profile  {:yin.lower/profile \\\\\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\\\\\"          ; the lowering profile, §5.2.1\\\\n                       :yin.code/contract \\\\\\\"v3\\\\\\\"  ; UCF S7.3.3 contract\\\\n                       :yin.k/version     0}}\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nIts address is published in the ledger (§8.3). The chain\\\\n`record → input, record → output` is what \\\\\\\"the hash chain is the ledger\\\\\\\"\\\\nmeans under this design: the chain lives in ledger records and refs, not\\\\nin the code content, which rightly excludes it.\\\\n\\\\n#### 5.2.1 The lowering profile\\\\n\\\\nUCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,\\\\nthe transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.\\\\nIts declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and\\\\ntwo deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two\\\\ndifferent, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable\\\\nonly against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:\\\\n\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Pinned by the profile                 | Reference value for `\\\\\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\\\\\"`                                                                                                 |\\\\n+=======================================+================================================================================================================================+\\\\n| input grammar                         | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document                                                                             |\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else                                                      |\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| emission rules                        | the §5.3 flattening table of [the deleted v3 design](./yin.vm.semantic.md#history), in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right)    |\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| label and body order                  | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were  |\\\\n|                                       | encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`)                                                  |\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| terminators                           | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`)                                          |\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| target execution contract             | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy                                                                          |\\\\n+---------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n\\\\nThe profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution\\\\ncontract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a\\\\nprofile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the\\\\nexecution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent\\\\nchoice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured\\\\nmap above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection\\\\n(§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.\\\\n\\\\n#### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps\\\\n\\\\nA consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two\\\\ndifferent things and reports them separately:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Content integrity.** The consumer verifies the claimed root ID against the root row and verifies each required row as `row.id == segment-key(row-body)`, recursively validating the reachable closure before reconstructing the semantic map. The instruction vector is verified similarly. Failure is\\\\n   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the value. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and\\\\n   says nothing about the derivation.\\\\n2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's\\\\n   `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it lowers the reconstructed map under that profile and verifies the resulting instruction-vector address against `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality\\\\n   verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-\\\\n   mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A\\\\n   consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports\\\\n   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` naming both profiles, the same outcome UCF\\\\n   §7.3.3 gives a different execution contract, and it may still *load* the\\\\n   output vector if the vector's own execution contract is one it\\\\n   implements, because content integrity (step 1) is independent of who\\\\n   lowered it.\\\\n  `:yin.module/contract \\\\\\\"v2\\\\\\\"` and would have refused every stack and\\\\n  register link of a valid manifest; that was a defect.\\\\n- **Verifying and trusting are two policies, and verifying never\\\\n  trusts.** The linker's `:derivation` policy is one of `:verifying` or\\\\n  `:trusted`, chosen by the composition; there is no policy under which\\\\n  a verifying link accepts a claim it did not recompute.\\\\n  - Under `:verifying`, for every image it delivers the linker fetches\\\\n    the manifest's tree through the `:yin.ast/code` record (steps 2 to\\\\n    4, so the tree itself is verified, and no further: the tree's own\\\\n    obligations are the walker's business, scanned only when the tree is\\\\n    the requested image), re-lowers it under the exact\\\\n    per-format profile the derivation record names (`\\\\\\\"ast-to-bytecode\\\\\\\"`\\\\n    for `:yin.semantic/code`, the stack lowering for\\\\n    `:yin.debruijn.code`, the register lowering for\\\\n    `:yin.debruijn.register`, each pinned by its own\\\\n    `:yin.ledger/profile` map), and compares the recomputed identity to\\\\n    `:yin.ledger/output` (`yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` section 5.2.2, step\\\\n    2). Inequality is `:derivation-mismatch`. If the linker does not\\\\n    implement that exact profile, or the tree is `:absent`, or the tree\\\\n    fails its own verification, the link is refused\\\\n    `:unverified-derivation` naming the profile and what was missing.\\\\n    It is never downgraded to trust.\\\\n  - Under `:trusted`, the linker checks only the record's input and\\\\n    output addresses (the rule above) and reports `:trust :composition`\\\\n    in the outcome, exactly as B6's trusted fallback did and never\\\\n    silently. A composition that chooses `:trusted` has chosen to\\\\n    accept the publisher's lowering, and the outcome says so.\\\\n    `:trusted` skips **re-lowering**, never retrievability: the\\\\n    composition that serves under `:trusted` holds the manifest's\\\\n    `:yin.module/tree` address in its `source-index` and is responsible\\\\n    for the tree being retrievable from it (the publisher's store, or a\\\\n    store it can reach); no fetch is performed by the policy, and a tree\\\\n    whose read fails later is the medium's `:absent`, not a policy\\\\n    violation (`yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md` §9.2).\\\\n  The B6 fallback pair maps onto these: `verifying-fallback` is the\\\\n  `:verifying` policy applied to an H image selected in place of an\\\\n  unsupported R; `trusted-fallback` is `:trusted` applied the same way.\\\\n  The `:yin.ast/code` image needs no derivation under either policy: it\\\\n  is the tree, verified by content.\\\\n- **The name is verified.** Immediately after the manifest passes steps\\\\n  2 to 4, the linker compares `:yin.module/name` with the\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_19\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '1205,1265p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; sed -n '180,222p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc; rg -n 'budget|baseline|instruction|trace capacity' docs/design/yang.typescript.md docs/design/yang.python.md docs/design/yang.php.md | head -50\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_19\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '1205,1265p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; sed -n '180,222p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc; rg -n 'budget|baseline|instruction|trace capacity' docs/design/yang.typescript.md docs/design/yang.python.md docs/design/yang.php.md | head -50\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"                      :syms (mapcat\\\\n                              (fn [i sym]\\\\n                                (for [[k v] (sort-by (comp pr-str key)\\\\n                                                     (apply dissoc (or (meta sym) {})\\\\n                                                            source-position-keys))]\\\\n                                  [[field i k] v]))\\\\n                              (range) value)\\\\n                      nil)))\\\\n                slots)]\\\\n    (concat own names)))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn ast-side-tables\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Project occurrence-owned frontend facts from a map AST without putting\\\\n   them in canonical rows. Returns the §2.5 relations\\\\n   `{:source-positions [[origin root path file line col] ...]\\\\n     :frontend-metadata [[origin root path key value] ...]}`.\\\\n\\\\n   `bc` must be the projection of `ast`; its root makes both relations join\\\\n   the canonical row and occurrence relations. Structural paths use the same\\\\n   row positions as `occurrences` and lowering.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [ast bc origin]\\\\n  (when-not (occurrence-origin? origin)\\\\n    (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Side tables require a ruled occurrence origin\\\\\\\"\\\\n                    {:rule :origin, :origin origin})))\\\\n  (let [root (:root bc)\\\\n        positions (atom [])\\\\n        metadata (atom [])]\\\\n    (letfn [(visit\\\\n              [node path]\\\\n              (let [tag (:type node)\\\\n                    slots (or (get semantic-bytecode-grammar tag)\\\\n                              (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Unknown AST node type\\\\\\\"\\\\n                                              {:type tag, :node node})))\\\\n                    position (reader-position node)]\\\\n                (when position\\\\n                  (swap! positions conj\\\\n                         [origin root path (:file position) (:line position)\\\\n                          (:column position)]))\\\\n                (doseq [[k v] (frontend-meta-entries tag node)]\\\\n                  (when-not (and (plain-data? k) (plain-data? v))\\\\n                    (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Frontend metadata is not plain data\\\\\\\"\\\\n                                    {:rule :frontend-metadata, :path path,\\\\n                                     :key k, :value v})))\\\\n                  (swap! metadata conj [origin root path k v]))\\\\n                (doseq [[j [field kind]] (map-indexed vector slots)\\\\n                        :let [pos (+ j 2)\\\\n                              value (get node field)]]\\\\n                  (case kind\\\\n                    :node (visit value (conj path pos))\\\\n                    :nodes (doseq [[i child] (map-indexed vector value)]\\\\n                             (visit child (conj path [pos i])))\\\\n                    nil))))]\\\\n      (visit ast [])\\\\n      {:source-positions @positions, :frontend-metadata @metadata})))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn- semantic-bytecode-slot-kind-ok?\\\\n  \\\\\\\"§2.2/§7.4 `:slot-kind`: whether `v` conforms to slot `kind`.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  [kind v]\\\\n  (case kind\\\\n  [rows]\\\\n  {:tag (fn [id] (nth (get rows id) 1)),\\\\n   :slot (fn [id f]\\\\n           (let [row (get rows id)]\\\\n             (nth row (get slot-position [(nth row 1) f]))))\\\\n   :check (fn [_id])})\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn project-rows\\\\n  \\\\\\\"Project one canonical row set (`vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`'s `{:root\\\\n   id, :rows {id row}}`) to the register-shaped vector. The rows are\\\\n   validated (§7.4) first and a defect throws naming it. `origin` is nil\\\\n   or one of §2.5's ruled occurrence origins.\\\\n\\\\n   Returns `{:vector v :address A :provenance [[pc origin root path] …]}`,\\\\n   one provenance row per pc, `path` being the emitting node's §2.5\\\\n   structural path from the root row (row-position steps; a `nodes` item\\\\n   is a `[position i]` pair); a body's `:return` carries its lambda's\\\\n   path.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  ([bc] (project-rows bc nil))\\\\n  ([{:keys [root rows], :as bc} origin]\\\\n   (when-not (or (nil? origin) (vm/occurrence-origin? origin))\\\\n     (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Cannot project rows with a malformed occurrence origin\\\\\\\"\\\\n                     {:rule :origin, :origin origin})))\\\\n   (when-let [{:keys [rule path id]} (vm/validate-rows bc)]\\\\n     (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"Cannot project rows that fail validation\\\\\\\"\\\\n                     (cond-> {:rule rule}\\\\n                       path (assoc :path path)\\\\n                       id (assoc :id id)))))\\\\n   (let [{:keys [code sources]} (flatten-tree (row-reader rows) root)]\\\\n     {:vector code,\\\\n      :address (ucf/code-address code),\\\\n      :provenance (mapv (fn [pc [_ path]] [pc origin root path])\\\\n                        (range)\\\\n                        sources)})))\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n(defn project\\\\n  \\\\\\\"`project-rows` ∘ `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode`: a map AST to the\\\\n   register-shaped vector by the row lane.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  ([ast] (project ast nil))\\\\n  ([ast origin] (project-rows (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast) origin)))\\\\n\\\\nrg: docs/design/yang.python.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1239:|    |                                    | the VM's operational halts (budget |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1930:  budget** instead, raising the fatal with PHP's text, a recorded departure\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1932:  integer budget per run; omission means unlimited. Each entry to a `:loop` or\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1937:  blocking host operations; no generic VM instructions are charged.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2102:|       | `pcntl_signal`, the safepoint    | stream never parks; budget        |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2103:|       | budget                           | exhaustion raises the pinned      |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2326:|                     | deterministic safepoint budget and  |                  |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2483:5. (B8.5) The deterministic safepoint budget as the `max_execution_time`\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_20\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_20\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_21\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_21\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_22\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_22\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_23\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The versioning obligations are also clearer: evidence schemas, lowering profiles, execution contracts, and module-manifest schemas are separate. Astra is checking which changes the addendum should require, while preserving the oracle round and current test lanes.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_24\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_24\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"collab_tool_call\\\",\\\"tool\\\":\\\"wait\\\",\\\"sender_thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\",\\\"receiver_thread_ids\\\":[],\\\"prompt\\\":null,\\\"agents_states\\\":{},\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_25\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"**gpt-6-astra’s verdict: amend before owner signature.** The direction is endorsed; the draft needs four clarifications.\\\\n\\\\n1. **Attach provenance to UAST application occurrences; do not yet prescribe a canonical node field.** Existing architecture defines a call site as `[origin root-address path]` and excludes provenance from hashed code content. Occurrence side tables and instruction-provenance mappings already exist. The draft’s representation choice conflicts with that boundary. See [occurrence identity](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:659).\\\\n\\\\n   Replace item 2 with:\\\\n\\\\n   > Every frontend preserves caller-site provenance for post-expansion UAST application occurrences through a shared, language-neutral occurrence contract. The design round starts from existing occurrence and instruction-provenance relations. This addendum does not authorize a new canonical AST slot; any such proposal requires explicit justification and amendment of the occurrence/content boundary.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Separate the versioning obligations.** “A §8.1 contract revision” is too broad. Evidence schemas, lowering profiles, execution contracts, and module-manifest schemas have distinct revision rules. Existing machinery detects incompatibility; it does not automatically migrate artifacts. See [§8.1](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1871) and [lowering profiles](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:861).\\\\n\\\\n   Require the design round to inventory affected producers, validators, lowering and reconstruction paths, derivation verification, publication, persisted artifacts, and mismatch behavior. Revise only the contracts actually changed.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Make adoption coverage and host verification explicit.** Include production `yang.clojure`: oracle instrumentation does not establish coverage of ordinary compilation paths. Inventory other `yang.*` entry points. Jolt inherits shared implementation changes, but still requires an applicable load/parity gate.\\\\n\\\\n   Replace the rollout wording with:\\\\n\\\\n   > Adoption covers production Clojure, TypeScript, PHP, and Python, with additional entry points inventoried during design. Each frontend has an acceptance gate; applicable JVM, Node, Dart, and Jolt host lanes verify the resulting revision. Current sweeps, the J2 rerun, and the oracle round continue under their existing scopes.\\\\n\\\\n   Also describe instantiation #1 as **oracle-specific instrumentation**, since its signed scope already permits walker changes.\\\\n\\\\n4. **Activate R1 at signature and establish a precise dependency.** Owner scheduling validly activates R1; an already shipped second consumer is unnecessary. However, “lands with (2)/(3)” leaves acceptance ordering unclear.\\\\n\\\\n   Replace that wording with:\\\\n\\\\n   > By signing this addendum, the owner activates R1’s deferred work. The design round specifies the durable correspondence record and its additive relationship to versioned oracle evidence. Implementation and independent reconstruction complete before, or atomically with, acceptance of the first additional consumer that relies on it. The oracle round remains unblocked.\\\\n\\\\nThe design round should explicitly measure application counts, provenance and trace sizes, peak trace occupancy, instruction overhead, compilation/loading/query time, and memory. Separate static annotation costs from runtime tracing costs, and declare bounds before tagged runs. Its correctness gates should cover duplicate occurrences, macro expansion, helper calls, indirect and tail calls, and applicable suspension/resumption paths.\\\\n\\\\n**With these amendments, Astra endorses the addendum for owner signature as architectural direction and scheduling authority.** R1–R7 remain intact. No files were changed; this was a read-only review.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"turn.completed\\\",\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1100731,\\\"cached_input_tokens\\\":1038208,\\\"cache_write_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":3609,\\\"reasoning_output_tokens\\\":530}}\\nDISPATCH_RC=0\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-RECORD.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/publication.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/publication.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc\\ncollab/yang-python/1793410000000-orchestrator-yang-python-attribution-note.md\\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/case_arms.edn\\ntest/yang/latte_oracle/throw_sites.edn\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:12:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1793954629000-orchestrator-jolt-to-yangclojure-reader-conditional-fix.md collab/yang-clojure/1793952867000-orchestrator-jolt-to-yangclojure-j2-probe-results.md docs/agents/build-n-test.md .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-06 08:43:49 UTC\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-06 15:43:49 +07\\nCoding-Agent: agy\\nModel: gemini-3.8-flash\\nSeat: yin.repl-jolt orchestrator\\n\\n# To: yang.clojure orchestrator\\n# Re: `yang.clojure` load under Jolt — explicit `:jolt` feature needed before `:clj`\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nWe re-tested `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc` under Jolt on current `master` (`7fc6024a`, which includes `acbf8e10` and `873c8bcd`).\\n`yang.clojure` still fails to load on Jolt with:\\n```\\nUnhandled exception (FileNotFoundException): Could not locate clojure/tools/reader.jolt (or .clj/.cljc) on the source roots\\n  at ./src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1:1\\n```\\n\\n## Why it happens\\n\\nIn `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:31`:\\n```clojure\\n  (:require #?(:cljd nil\\n               :clj [clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\\n               :cljs [cljs.tools.reader :as treader])\\n            #?(:cljd nil\\n               :clj [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]\\n               :cljs [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt])\\n            ...)\\n```\\n\\nWhen Jolt's reader processes `#?`, it includes `:clj` in its fallback feature set if `:jolt` is not matched first:\\n```clojure\\n(read-string \\\"#?(:clj :clj-val :default :def-val)\\\")        ;; => :clj-val\\n(read-string \\\"#?(:jolt :jolt-val :clj :clj-val :default)\\\") ;; => :jolt-val\\n```\\n\\nBecause `:jolt` is not listed in `#?(:cljd ... :clj ...)`, Jolt takes the `:clj` branch and attempts to load `clojure.tools.reader`, which fails because maven jar dependencies are not on Jolt's classpath.\\n\\nFurthermore, if `#?(:jolt nil ...)` is used inside `(:require ...)`, Jolt reads `(:require nil ...)`, which is invalid syntax for some Clojure tools and loaders.\\n\\n## Recommended Fix\\n\\nUse reader splicing `#?@` with an explicit `:jolt []` preceding `:clj`:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(ns yang.clojure\\n  ...\\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [compile])\\n  (:require #?@(:jolt []\\n                :cljd []\\n                :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\\n                      [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]]\\n                :cljs [[cljs.tools.reader :as treader]\\n                       [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]])\\n            [yang.core.call :as call]\\n            [yang.core.uast :as u]\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\\n```\\n\\nWe verified this directly with Jolt:\\n```clojure\\n(read-string \\\"(:require #?@(:jolt [] :cljd [] :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]]))\\\")\\n;; => (:require)\\n```\\nThis cleanly omits `clojure.tools.reader` under Jolt without emitting `nil` tokens into `(:require ...)`, while preserving the required libspecs on JVM and Node.\\n\\nOnce you land this fix, we will re-run the Jolt verification probe and confirm the J2 load path.\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-06 08:14:27 UTC\\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-06 15:14:27 +07\\nCoding-Agent: agy\\nModel: gemini-3.8-flash\\nSeat: yin.repl-jolt orchestrator\\n\\n# To: yang.clojure orchestrator (cc: dao.stream.ble seat)\\n# Re: J2 probe / load check results on master (acbf8e10 / 873c8bcd) & J2 manifest failure\\n\\n## 1. Jolt load check on `yang.clojure` is still FAILING\\n\\nWe ran the requested J2 load verification on current master head for `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`.\\n\\nUnder Jolt (`jolt -Srepro -M:jolt`):\\n```clojure\\n(require 'yang.clojure)\\n;; => FileNotFoundException: Could not locate clojure/tools/reader.jolt (or .clj/.cljc) on the source roots\\n;;    at src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1:1\\n```\\n\\n### Root Cause Analysis\\n\\nCommit `acbf8e10` moved the reader dependencies back inside `(:require ...)` in the `ns` declaration:\\n```clojure\\n  (:require #?(:cljd nil\\n               :clj [clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\\n               :cljs [cljs.tools.reader :as treader])\\n            #?(:cljd nil\\n               :clj [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]\\n               :cljs [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt])\\n            [yang.core.call :as call]\\n            [yang.core.uast :as u]\\n            [yin.vm :as vm]))\\n```\\n\\nIn Jolt's reader, `#?` conditionals evaluate against `(:features *clojure-version*)`.\\nWhen Jolt reads `#?(:clj ...)`, because Jolt identifies as a Clojure dialect targeting native Chez, `:clj` matches when `:jolt` is not explicitly declared first! Concretely:\\n```clojure\\n;; In Jolt:\\n(read-string \\\"#?(:clj :clj-val :default :def-val)\\\")\\n;; => :clj-val\\n\\n(read-string \\\"#?(:jolt :jolt-val :clj :clj-val :default :def-val)\\\")\\n;; => :jolt-val\\n```\\n\\nBecause `acbf8e10` and `873c8bcd` omitted an explicit `:jolt` feature match before `:clj`, Jolt falls through to the `:clj` branch and attempts to load `clojure.tools.reader`, which fails because maven jar dependencies are not on Jolt's source-root classpath.\\n\\nFurthermore, inside `(:require ...)` in an `ns` declaration, placing `nil` (e.g. `#?(:jolt nil :clj ...)`) results in `(:require nil ...)` which some Clojure readers reject.\\nSplicing reader conditionals `#?@` work cleanly across Clojure readers:\\n```clojure\\n  (:require #?@(:jolt []\\n                :cljd []\\n                :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]\\n                      [clojure.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]]\\n                :cljs [[cljs.tools.reader :as treader]\\n                       [cljs.tools.reader.reader-types :as rt]])\\n            [yang.core.call :as call]\\n            ...)\\n```\\nIn Jolt:\\n```clojure\\n(read-string \\\"(:require #?@(:jolt [] :clj [[clojure.tools.reader :as treader]]))\\\")\\n;; => (:require)\\n```\\nThis avoids splicing `nil` into `(:require ...)` and ensures `:jolt` is matched before `:clj`.\\n\\nPlease consider this fix on your side so the Jolt lane and J2 load can turn green.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. FYI: J2 Manifest Failure from unlisted BLE tests\\n\\nThe full J2 lane driver (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core`) currently fails immediately at the manifest guard:\\n```\\nJ2 manifest FAILED:\\nUNLISTED dao.stream.ble-test -- test/dao/stream/ble_test.cljc\\nUNLISTED dao.stream.ble.dart-test -- test/dao/stream/ble/dart_test.cljd\\nUNLISTED dao.stream.ble.links-test -- test/dao/stream/ble/links_test.cljc\\nUNLISTED dao.stream.ble.mesh-test -- test/dao/stream/ble/mesh_test.cljc\\n```\\nCommit `c0e2f8d4` landed BLE tests under `test/dao/stream/ble*` without adding them to `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`'s inclusion or exclusion manifest.\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# When a test fails, reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the failing test\\n# (`clojure -M:test -n <ns> -v <ns>/<var>`, Node `--test=<ns>`, Dart `--only <ns>`) --\\n# never the whole suite (owner ruling 2026-11-01; see Gate Ladder G0).\\n# Individual tests run fine on the laptop; suite-scale runs (G1 subsystem sets,\\n# G2 changed sets, full suites, slow tier, nightly) run on shiva/jupiter --\\n# the suite has outgrown the laptop (owner ruling 2026-11-04).\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` runs once daily on the remote hosts (04:00 VN);\\n# a MERGE needs only the affected tests (bb test:changed) -- owner ruling 2026-11-01.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\\n```\\n\\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\\n`--changed FILE...` or `--base REF` uses `affected/select` and\\n`affected/lane-tests`, exactly as `test:changed:clj` does. Wide input selects\\nthe full JVM set here. It never changes `test:changed`'s selection or exit-2\\nsemantics. `--workers N` must be positive (default 4 on the laptop).\\n\\nShard membership comes from every entry in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`, using\\n`affected/select-subsystems` and the same longest-prefix ownership rule as\\n`test:sub`. As in the shiva script below, currently unowned namespaces run\\nin an explicit `unmapped` residual shard, printed by name. Registry omissions\\ntherefore cannot silently shrink coverage. Each nonempty shard gets its own\\n`clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns> ...` process, with at most N JVMs running.\\nPrerequisites are derived from the entire selected JVM reach and finish\\nserially before workers start; the Node REPL build prerequisite\\n(`build:yin-repl-node`) also ensures `bb deps:npm`.\\n\\nEach invocation writes a separate `target/profile/quick-sharded-<id>/`:\\n`selected.txt`, `ns-<shard>.txt`, `covered.txt`, `uncovered.txt`, per-shard logs,\\nand `summary.edn`. Before execution it checks **set equality** between selected\\nnamespaces and the union of shard lists, plus absence of duplicate assignments.\\nAny uncovered, unexpected, or duplicate namespace fails loudly before tests.\\nThe final summary totals tests, assertions, failures and errors and separates\\nJVM wall time from total time including selection/builds. A nonzero child exit,\\nfailure/error, or missing final test-runner summary makes the task exit nonzero.\\nDry plans write namespace evidence but execute no builds or tests. An empty\\nselection prints `0 JVM namespaces selected` and exits 1, including dry plans.\\nThe final `summary.edn` includes a `:verification-scope` record; the same EDN\\nrecord is printed on stdout with `:type :verification-scope`, `:runner`,\\n`:scope`, `:exit`, `:dry-run?`, and `:gate-complete?`. JVM feedback always has\\n`:scope :jvm-feedback` and `:gate-complete? false`, even on exit 0.\\n\\n`test:routed <changed-files...>` applies this priority table to the whole change\\nset, prints the exact dispatch, executes it and propagates its result:\\n\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Change class (first match wins)                                                              | Dispatch                                                              |\\n+==============================================================================================+=======================================================================+\\n| Existing wide inputs (`deps.edn`, `bb.edn`, `src/dev/`, ANTLR, package locks, runner         | `bb test` — full quick tri-host; success required                     |\\n| configs, etc.), toolchain manifests, CI workflows, build scripts                             |                                                                       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Only `.md`, `.txt`, `.rst` documentation, excluding test resources                           | `git diff --check HEAD -- <paths>` — documentation whitespace/format  |\\n|                                                                                              | lint only                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Foundation code owned by a layer-0 entry in `subsystems.edn`, or `yang.clojure`, `yang.php`, | `bb test:quick-sharded --changed <paths>` — broad selected JVM        |\\n| `yang.typescript`, `yang.python` code                                                        | feedback                                                              |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other code/tests/fixtures with a nonempty affected selection                                 | `bb test:changed --changed <paths>` — existing quick tri-host         |\\n|                                                                                              | selection                                                             |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Other non-doc input with no statically selected tests                                        | `bb test` — fail closed rather than report a vacuous green gate       |\\n+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\nMixed changes take the widest matching route. Missing paths, an empty git change\\nset, unknown flags, and the wrong cwd fail. Deleted paths are validated against\\nthe base tree. If the leaf command returns exit 2, routing immediately dispatches\\n`bb test` and requires its success. `--dry-run`/`--list` only reports the route;\\nits zero exit is **not** a verification result. Documentation checks inspect\\nstaged and unstaged changes together against HEAD. Paths absent from\\n`git ls-files` print `unchecked (untracked)` and appear in the scope record\\nunder `:unchecked`; they cannot complete the docs gate. These checks do not lint\\nexecutable examples or validate documentation links.\\n\\nRouting prints its own final EDN verification-scope record. `:gate-complete?`\\nmeans the dispatched gate completed successfully, not that every landing gate\\nis satisfied. It is false for foundation/JVM feedback, dry runs, unchecked docs,\\nand failed dispatches. Successful checked docs, selected tri-host, and full\\nquick tri-host routes report `:docs-whitespace`, `:selected-quick-tri-host`, and\\n`:full-quick-tri-host` respectively. Exit codes continue to propagate child\\nresults, so automation must inspect the scope as well as the exit code.\\n\\n**Gate requirements are unchanged.** Sharded JVM success alone is not G2/G3\\ntri-host success: for foundation/compiler changes also run\\n`bb test:changed:cljs --changed <paths>` and\\n`bb test:changed:cljd --changed <paths>`, respecting the existing JVM/Dart\\nserialization rule. G1, full post-merge `bb test` (G3), applicable slow G4,\\ndemo smoke checks and review/landing gates all remain required. Node and Dart\\nlanes retain their current commands. The original unsharded tasks remain\\navailable. **Individual tests may run on the laptop; suite-scale runs may\\nnot (owner ruling 2026-11-04).** A single failing test or one namespace\\nreproduces quickly and is fine locally. Everything suite-scale — subsystem\\nlane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), the full quick suite, the slow tier,\\n`bb test:all`, and `nightly.clj` — runs on the remote hosts: the suite has\\noutgrown the laptop. The slow tier is 52 long namespaces whose JVM\\nprocesses each multi-thread (test workers, JIT, GC threads sized by core\\ncount); four of them GC-thrashing at once drove a laptop load average over\\n100 on 2026-11-01 while making no progress (the remote hosts run this tier:\\njupiter 5,645 s at 12 workers, shiva comparable). Do not run another lane\\nset concurrently on the laptop either.\\n\\n#### Worktree-local prerequisite caches\\n\\n`bb gen:antlr` now checks a content key over all grammar/manifests (including\\nrecorded helper/generated digests and runtime pins), the generator/cache source,\\n`deps.edn`, `mise.toml`, actual Java/javac versions and canonical worktree path.\\nIt also verifies the names and SHA-256 bytes of all generated targets, compiled\\nclasses and cached upstream helpers before a hit. Changed inputs or missing,\\nremoved or corrupted outputs trigger a full generation, retaining the generator's\\ndigest checks. Filtered/discovery invocations retain their CLI behavior and\\ninvalidate the complete-build stamp.\\n\\n`bb deps:npm` wraps `npm ci` with a key covering `package-lock.json`,\\n`package.json`, npm configuration, Node/npm versions, platform, relevant npm/Node\\nenvironment and worktree identity; it verifies the installed file tree before\\nreuse. A failed command never establishes a cache hit. Existing Node build tasks\\nensure this dependency too. Direct `npm ci` is still available.\\n\\nStamps and fail-fast writer locks live under `target/prereq-cache/`. Remove a\\nlock only after confirming its owning process died. Symlinked output parents\\nthat escape the worktree are refused. This is reuse **within one worktree**,\\nnot a shared artifact store: never share mutable `build/`, `lib/`, `target/` or\\n`node_modules/` across live worktrees. ANTLR generation and Dart compilation\\nstill require the existing worktree isolation/serialization rules; the cache\\ndoes not make overlapping builds and tests safe. Peer executables and shadow\\nbuilds themselves are not cached by this mechanism.\\n\\nLaptop pilot (2026-10-29, 8 cores, 4 workers, `yang.clojure.cljc` changed):\\nthe unchanged selector and the sharded plan both covered **61 JVM namespaces**;\\nboth completed with **955 tests, 14,848 assertions, zero failures/errors**.\\nSharded JVM wall time was **177.165 s** (total including prerequisites **257.207 s**;\\nouter command **258.27 s**). The original `test:changed:clj` command completed\\nin **376.79 s** overall and reported **298 s** for its JVM lane (that runner\\nrounds to whole seconds). The investigation's earlier unsharded command had\\ntimed out at 300 s without a summary. These are single-run observations, not\\na median/p95 claim or a replacement for tri-host landing verification.\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n   **Failure-reproduction rule (owner ruling 2026-11-01): when a test fails anywhere — a\\n   gate, the nightly, or a delegate report — reproduce and fix it by running ONLY the\\n   failing test, never the whole suite.** JVM: `clojure -M:test -n <ns>` (a single\\n   deftest: add `-v <ns>/<var-name>`). Node: `node target/node-tests.js --test=<ns>`\\n   (built by `bb build:yin-repl-node` first only if that test needs the Node REPL).\\n   Dart: `bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj --only <ns>`. Fix against the failing test alone,\\n   re-run it green, then climb back up (G1 for the home subsystem, G2 for dependents).\\n   Never reproduce or chase a failure with the full suite or the slow tier — the full\\n   suite is not a debugging tool, and running it locally for one failing test is the\\n   exact misuse that drove a laptop load average over 100 (2026-11-01). A failure that\\n   only reproduces under the full suite's concurrency is diagnosed with the suite's own\\n   logs (its per-shard/per-test output), not by re-running the suite repeatedly.\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master (merged in place by the 2026-11-01 owner ruling)**: The landing gate\\n   for a change is the **tests that cover the feature being built** — `bb test:changed`\\n   (G2) already includes the reverse-require closure of the changed files, and a wide\\n   change still routes to the full quick lane. The complete suite is no longer a\\n   per-merge gate: it runs twice a day on the remote hosts (see \\\"Cadence\\\" below), so a\\n   break is caught within hours without charging every merge ~400 minutes of slow tier.\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge\\n   (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n   (owner decision 2026-10-12), and implicitly twice daily by the scheduled run below.\\n   G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence (owner ruling 2026-11-05):** a merge needs only the tests that cover the\\n  feature being built — the affected/dependent set from `bb test:changed` (or\\n  `bb test:sub <own> --slow` when the change touches slow-tagged code). The FULL suite,\\n  including the slow tier, runs **once a day at 04:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh** as a\\n  disjoint lane pair (`src/dev/nightly.clj`): **jupiter takes the jvm lane, shiva\\n  takes node and dart** (each lane in its own clone; shiva publishes the run pin,\\n  jupiter reads it and falls back to its own current master as an advisory run).\\n  The measured long set is ~400 minutes of serial slow-tier work, so it cannot be\\n  a per-merge cost; a break is instead caught by the next scheduled run.\\n  The schedule is an **hourly cron plus a Vietnam-time guard** in\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on each host (the guard fires only at the 04 VN hour and\\n  `flock` skips a slot whose previous run is still active), so it is immune to the\\n  DST shifts of either host's local timezone. **The cron entries are currently\\n  REMOVED (owner instruction 2026-11-06)**: the wrappers remain installed on both\\n  hosts and re-arming is one crontab line each\\n  (`0 * * * * ~/ci/nightly-cron.sh >> ~/ci/nightly-cron.log 2>&1`). Logs:\\n  `~/ci/nightly-cron.log`; results: `~/ci/nightly-results/<stamp>/summary.txt`.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (server snapshot checked 2026-10-11; repo selection comes from `mise.toml`) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Server snapshot: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1. The repo's `mise.toml` selects Flutter `latest` on beta, not a fixed version. Activate the repo toolchain before running any lane. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | Server snapshot (rechecked 2026-10-29): java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.8 (the stale hand-symlinked 3.41.9 was replaced via `mise install flutter@3.47.8` after it failed to compile `dart:ui`-using cljd namespaces; if the Dart lane dies in the cljd compile, check `mise ls flutter` first). Tri-host quick tier verified green on 2026-10-29 (see Measured times). |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Kill remote processes by exact PID, never by name pattern\\n\\nOn these hosts, kill a runaway nightly/lane process by its exact PID\\n(`pgrep -af <pattern>` only to LIST, then `kill <pid>`), never with\\n`pkill -f <string>` / `killall <name>`. Measured 2026-11-06: `pkill -f\\n\\\"nightly.clj\\\"` over SSH matched the invoking bash command line itself\\n(the pattern appears in `ps` output of the session carrying the kill),\\nterminating the SSH session mid-command — exit 255 with the kill\\nhalf-done. A name-pattern kill also races concurrent seats: another\\norchestrator's delegate process whose arguments embed the same string\\ndies with yours. If the PID from `pgrep -af` is ambiguous (several\\ncandidates), inspect with `ps -o pid,etime,args -p <pid>` first and kill\\nthe exact process; a session that dies under you is a symptom of this\\nmistake, not a host outage.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### A clone is only current if it updated from real master\\n\\nA scratch clone's `origin/master` ref is only as fresh as the place it fetches\\nfrom — and a clone made from another scratch clone inherits that clone's *stale*\\nrefs. Measured on shiva 2026-11-02: the `dg` clone was created from the `p4`\\nscratch clone, whose local `master` ref had not moved since October 26 because\\nevery lane on it ran detached off fetched branches; the `dg` lane's\\n`checkout origin/master` therefore produced a commit 436 commits behind real\\nmaster, and the G3 Dart leg honestly reported failures (the datagram fix) for a\\ntree that did not contain them.\\n\\nBefore any lane runs in a clone — every time, not just the first:\\n\\n```sh\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\ngit remote get-url origin          # must be the GitHub URL, never another scratch clone\\ngit fetch -q origin \\\"+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*\\\"\\ngit checkout -q --detach origin/master   # or the lane's branch\\ngit merge-base --is-ancestor <the-commit-hash-you-pushed> HEAD && echo CURRENT\\n```\\n\\nIf `origin` is not GitHub, repoint it (`git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\"`). If\\nthe `merge-base` check fails, the clone's bookkeeping lies: refetch, or delete\\nand recreate the clone from GitHub directly. Never trust a hash the clone\\nreports without comparing it to the hash your push from the laptop printed.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\nmise install                                    # install versions selected by this checkout's mise.toml\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-selected tools on PATH\\nmise current flutter                            # verify the selected Flutter version\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-selected tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). The laptop additionally never runs the slow tier or the nightly\\ndriver (see the sharded-lane section) — a full slow tier there reached a load\\naverage over 100 on 2026-11-01; the remote hosts own that tier. On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n**The nightly driver now enforces this by construction.** `src/dev/nightly.clj`\\n(2026-11-03, kimi-reviewed) runs a multi-lane invocation as one concurrent\\nfuture per lane, each in its OWN clone — jvm keeps the primary clone, node\\nand dart get `<primary>-node` / `<primary>-dart`. The pinned SHA is resolved\\nonce and checked out in every lane clone; a cache-warm pass\\n(`clojure -P -M:test:cljs:clojuredart:cljd`) runs in the primary before\\nfan-out so two clones never fetch into the shared `~/.m2`/`~/.gitlibs`\\nconcurrently; per-lane summaries are appended serially after the futures\\njoin. A single-lane invocation (jupiter's `jvm`) is unchanged: one clone,\\none serial lane. Both hosts run the same driver — the lane list on the\\ncommand line (shiva: `node,dart` + `--pin-publish`; jupiter: `jvm` +\\n`--pin-read --pin-peer shiva...`) is the only difference.\\n\\n### Scratch-clone first-run gaps (two measured sightings)\\n\\nA fresh scratch clone needs two things the lane runner does not yet set up\\nitself; until the runner heuristic is fixed, do them by hand before the\\nfirst lane:\\n\\n1. **`npm install`** — the Node tests require npm deps (`@noble/hashes` was\\n   the measured miss on shiva 2026-11-02: the cljs lane ran but the\\n   node-tests bundle died with `MODULE_NOT_FOUND`).\\n2. **`bb gen:antlr`** — the `prerequisites` heuristic in\\n   `src/dev/affected.clj` decides `:gen:antlr` from the JVM lane's reach\\n   only (\\\"ANTLR classes are JVM only\\\" — stale: python keeps multi-target\\n   parsers), so a cljs/cljd-only selection that reaches\\n   `yang.python.antlr.parser` (e.g. `bb test:sub:cljs yang.base` via\\n   `yin.repl.frontends`) skips the generation and the shadow build dies on\\n   the missing `Python3Lexer.js`. Sighted independently on the p4 lane\\n   (PhpLexer ClassNotFoundException) and the yang.base cljs lane\\n   (2026-11-02). The lane-runner fix landed 2026-11-03 (eb27fd47).\\n\\n### Merge verification: run the suite from a fresh clone, not the authoring worktree\\n\\nA merge whose new code reads a file that was never `git add`ed passes in\\nthe authoring worktree (the untracked file is still on disk there) and\\nbreaks everywhere else. Measured: the H3-0 landing (1fd7236b) merged\\n`kernel-declarations` — which reads\\n`yang/latte_oracle/slice9_declarations.edn` via `io/resource` at load —\\nwithout the file (untracked in the authoring worktree); master's\\nyang.latte-oracle-test died at load until the file was restored\\n(2026-11-04). Before landing any merge that adds code reading a new\\nresource, or after any merge at all when in doubt:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit worktree add /tmp/merge-verify <merge-commit>\\ncd /tmp/merge-verify && <the touched subsystem's focused test>\\ngit worktree remove /tmp/merge-verify\\n```\\n\\nA fresh clone/worktree has only committed files — a missing untracked\\nresource fails there immediately.\\n\\n### Ledger commit hygiene: never commit a conflict marker\\n\\nThe append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`,\\n`orchestrator-logs/*.md`, `docs/agents/routing-status.md`) resolve rebase\\nand merge conflicts by the union rule, which means conflicts are common —\\nand a conflict marker that reaches a commit silently corrupts the record\\n(measured: the 2026-10-30 rebase landed a log with three committed\\n`<<<<<<<`/`>>>>>>>` markers and dropped records the union was supposed to\\nkeep; caught by the architect four rounds later). Before committing ANY\\nchange to a ledger file:\\n\\n```sh\\ngit diff --cached | grep -E '^\\\\+(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' && echo CONFLICT-MARKERS-STAGED\\ngit grep -E '^(<{7}|={7}$|>{7})' -- docs/agents/known-gaps.md orchestrator-logs docs/agents/routing-status.md\\n```\\n\\nIf either hits, the commit stops until the union is redone. When resolving\\na ledger conflict, prefer regenerating the union from both sides' section\\nlists over hand-merging, and re-read the result against BOTH parents before\\nstaging.\\n\\n### The jolt J2 lane: run it with `--jobs` on the servers\\n\\nThe jolt lane's core mode (`bb src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj core [--jobs N]\\n[--only re] [--timeout-ms M]`) runs each test namespace as its own child jolt\\nprocess (own Chez VM, own native parser library load, own log under\\n`build/j2-logs/core/`), so the namespaces cannot corrupt one another — the\\nonly thing `--jobs N` overlaps is the waiting. The default is `jobs 1`\\n(strictly serial), which on jupiter makes the full ~179-namespace lane a\\n5-6-hour run.\\n\\nOn the servers, run it with `--jobs 16`:\\n\\n- jolt children are essentially single-threaded (the 16 MiB parse thread\\n  only burns during parses) and cost 1-2 GB each, so 16 concurrent children\\n  sit far inside jupiter's 48 cores / 62 GB and shiva's 24 / 62;\\n- the lane's wall time is dominated by a few long namespaces\\n  (`yin.repl.python-antlr-test` has an explicit 3-hour step,\\n  `yin.vm.semantic.safepoint-harness-test` needs one — see\\n  `step-timeout-ms` in `src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj`), so beyond ~16 workers\\n  the critical path is the longest child, not the count;\\n- **parallel is unproven until checked once**: the recorded lane evidence\\n  (J2 closure, the safepoint 60/265,225/0 pass) was all produced `jobs 1`.\\n  The first parallel run must be compared per-namespace against a serial\\n  baseline; any namespace that flips red gets one serial re-run to\\n  attribute before its evidence is trusted. Until that first comparison\\n  passes, keep `jobs 1` for anything whose verdict is timing-sensitive —\\n  timeout and wall-time measurements, native-memory/leak gates, and the\\n  merge-decision qualification runs.\\n\\nSuites that exceed the lane's default step budget are pinned in the lane's\\n`step-timeout-ms` map with their measured wall time; a namespace timing out\\nunder the default budget is a reason to measure it serially and add an\\nentry, not to re-run under the same bound.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (both hosts run all three lanes; a second clone for Dart is recommended)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` on Linux —\\n  MEASURED AND FIXED (2026-11-02).** The old entry inferred that \\\"a second UDP bind on\\n  a taken port behaves differently on Linux\\\"; confirmed, with the cause. Dart's\\n  `RawDatagramSocket.bind` defaults to `reuseAddress: true`, and **Linux honours\\n  `SO_REUSEADDR` for UDP**, so a second bind to a port a reuse-enabled socket already\\n  holds *succeeds* (`strace`: `SO_REUSEADDR=1` on both sockets, no `SO_REUSEPORT`, both\\n  kernel `bind()` calls succeed); macOS refuses with errno 48. The seam was correct\\n  throughout — given a genuinely exclusive holder (`reuseAddress: false`) it deposits\\n  `bind-failed` in ~10 ms with errno 98. The test asserted one host's binding policy\\n  rather than the contract, so it now binds its own exclusive blocker\\n  (`RawDatagramSocket.bind \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" 0 .reuseAddress false .reusePort false`) and\\n  points the seam at that port: the failure path is exercised on both hosts. Verified\\n  on shiva: the namespace passes 5/5.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### The nightly full suite and the branch-testing rule (2026-10-30 owner ruling)\\n\\nOwner, verbatim: \\\"since the complete test can take a good 7-8hrs to run\\ncompletely, it should be schedule to run once a day at 3am. while\\nbranch/worktree test should only run test that are targeting code\\nchanges\\\" — and, clarifying the nightly's target: \\\"it should not be for\\nonly a worktree/branch. the nightly complete test should be for master.\\\"\\n\\nTwo tiers, by target and purpose:\\n\\n- **The nightly full suite runs MASTER ONLY** (`~/ci/nightly-cron.sh` on\\n  shiva and jupiter, hourly cron plus a 04:00 Vietnam guard and flock,\\n  documented under \\\"Cadence\\\" above; the driver fetches and checks out\\n  origin/master before anything runs). Every lane, across the host pair:\\n  the JVM full suite on jupiter, the full Node and Dart suites on shiva.\\n  Its results are a\\n  post-hoc integrity net for master, NOT a landing gate. Failures it\\n  finds route to fix rounds the next morning.\\n- **Branch/worktree testing runs only the namespaces that target the\\n  changed code** (the diff's own tests plus the surfaces the diff\\n  touches), one JVM per namespace, plus one cheap portable lane (Node)\\n  when cljc goldens or fixtures moved. Never the full tier on a branch:\\n  a branch gate needs the changed code proven, not the whole tree\\n  re-proven. A branch is never promoted by its own testing — the merge\\n  to master is what puts it under the nightly.\\n\\nAn orchestrator landing a slice cites the subset runs plus the gate\\nsign-off; the nightly catches what the subset could not see, and a red\\nnightly owns the follow-up.\\n\\n**The merge precondition (2026-10-30 owner ruling, restored 2026-10-31 —\\na dedup pass dropped this section along with a stale duplicate of the\\ntwo-tier policy; both stand):** before any merge into master, the\\n`dao.stream` tests must run and pass — dao.stream is what everything is\\nbuilt on. Concretely: `bb test:sub dao.stream` (the subsystem's tests,\\n`--slow` when the slice touches slow-guarded surfaces) is part of every\\nbranch gate regardless of which subsystems the diff's own changes\\nselect, because a change can break the foundation without touching it.\\nA red dao.stream blocks the merge until fixed or diagnosed as\\npre-existing by the owning seat.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores, and measure before relying on a speedup. (Historical record — the\\n\\\"free laptop\\\" comparison predates the 2026-11-04 ruling that suite-scale runs\\nbelong on the remote hosts.)\\n\\n### Jupiter verification run (2026-10-29, commit 7c0a078f, scratch clone, sequential lanes)\\n\\nFirst full tri-host verification of jupiter. Prerequisites (npm ci, Node REPL,\\nANTLR via `gen:antlr`, Dart peer) all succeeded; the stale 3.41.9 Flutter was\\nreplaced with 3.47.8 before the Dart lane. Results: **JVM quick tier\\n(`test:quick-sharded`, 12 workers) rc=0 in 702 s** (cold, every shard green);\\n**Node quick tier rc=0 in 4583 s** (all tests pass, but roughly 15x the\\nlaptop — route Node lanes to shiva; the laptop comparison is historical and\\nsuite-scale Node runs no longer belong there per the 2026-11-04 ruling);\\n**Dart quick tier\\nrc=1 in 1495 s with the ONLY failure the documented known Linux-only red**\\n(`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events`, same\\ntaken-port/bind-failed signature recorded above). Routing summary: jupiter\\nis the preferred JVM/sharding box (48 cores); Node stays off it when wall\\ntime matters; Dart works on either host. Timings are single cold runs, not\\nmedians, and two foreign QEMU guests idle on the host.\\n\\nOne exception to the concurrency above: never run two lanes that both trigger\\n`gen:antlr` (the quick `test:cljs`/`test:cljd` tasks) in the SAME worktree at the\\nsame time — they race on `build/antlr` and `lib/antlr` and fail with\\n\\\"Patch does not apply\\\" or \\\"Generated sources differ from the recorded digests\\\".\\nRun them sequentially, or in separate clones (as the slow-tier harness does).\\nThe fix after a collision is `rm -rf build/antlr lib/antlr` and a rerun; the\\ngenerator is not idempotent over an already-patched tree. Corroborated twice in\\none week (the M3 round and the C3 completion round, 2026-10-18).\\n\\n### Remote agent execution (delegate CLIs on shiva and jupiter)\\n\\nOwner relay, 2026-10-24: the delegate agent CLIs — `claude`, `codex`, `glm` —\\nare installed and usable **on the remote hosts themselves**, not only from the\\norchestrator's laptop. This generalizes this section from \\\"run tests remotely\\nover SSH\\\" to \\\"delegate whole work agents remotely\\\": a heavy implementation or\\nverification leg (test lanes, native builds, fault-injection runs, multi-hour\\nsuites) can be executed by a delegate agent running on the server, keeping the\\norchestrator's laptop unloaded.\\n\\nThe pattern (composed from the rules above plus the delegate-invocation\\nreference):\\n\\n1. **Land the branch on origin first** (push from the laptop; never rsync) and\\n   prepare the scratch clone per \\\"Get the code there\\\".\\n2. **Start a named tmux session** for the task; run the agent inside it with\\n   the toolchain activated *inside* the tmux command (`eval \\\"$(mise env -s\\n   bash)\\\"` after the `cd` — the non-interactive SSH shell does not have it).\\n   A fresh clone hits mise's interactive trust prompt, which blocks the whole\\n   session until answered via `tmux send-keys`; pre-empt it with `mise trust`\\n   inside the same command.\\n3. **The remote agent works in the clone**: implement, build, run lanes,\\n   write its findings artifact — the same brief form as any delegate dispatch\\n   (role template, bounded file ownership, deliverable path), just executing\\n   on the host. Long jobs survive SSH drops because of tmux.\\n4. **The orchestrator polls detached files, never the pane** (pane captures\\n   carry control characters that make output look empty): rc files, summary\\n   logs and `ls *.flag` sentinels written by the run.\\n5. **Division of labor**: bookkeeping and polling are orchestrator SSH\\n   one-liners; execution belongs to the remote agent. Do not run heavy lanes\\n   from the orchestrator's laptop while a remote agent owns the same gate.\\n6. **Same trust rules as any delegate**: remote execution does not waive\\n   Workflow steps 6–7 of the orchestrator role — artifacts are verified, and\\n   review before commit applies to remote-produced work identically.\\n   Session-ID capture and resume rules from the delegate-invocation reference\\n   apply per host (each host's `~/.claude`, `~/.codex` store is its own).\\n\\nKnown state: `claude` and `codex` verified working on shiva (2026-10-24, the\\njolt canonical-fixes verification ran there); `glm` reported available by the\\nowner on both hosts, not yet exercised. Both hosts are toolchain-verified for\\nthe full tri-host suite (jupiter verified 2026-10-29 — see Measured times;\\nits 3.41.9 Flutter was replaced with 3.47.8, and model availability per\\nhost is governed by `routing-status.md`, not this file).\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\\n\\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\\n\\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\\n\\n### A. Logic & Correctness\\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\\n\\n### B. Security & Credentials\\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\\n\\n### C. Architecture & API Design\\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\\n\\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\\n\\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\\n\\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\\n2. **Explain the \\\"Why\\\"**: Don't just say \\\"change this\\\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \\\"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\\\").\\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794330000000-engineer-cljrt-r5.prompt.md:31:- REMOTE (the seat runs these on jupiter; list the exact commands): full matrix namespace, bb test:sub:clj yang.base, full yang.clojure-test, the 4M kernel gate deftest.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794065803000-engineer-stream-eval-summary-fix.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:108:## Verification 2 — jupiter JVM lane (isolated worktree at cb2cbe11)\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794065803000-engineer-stream-eval-summary-fix.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:110:Deviation, deliberate: jupiter's `~/ci/datomworld-nightly` is **detached at master 3d2f20db**, not cb2cbe11,\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794065803000-engineer-stream-eval-summary-fix.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:123:HOST=jupiter.stigmergy.systems\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794065803000-engineer-stream-eval-summary-fix.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:171:  jupiter, and the laptop for re-application.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794065803000-engineer-stream-eval-summary-fix.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log:172:- **jupiter**: `~/ci/datomworld-nightly` untouched (still detached at 3d2f20db, clean). Scratch worktree left\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1793953025937-reviewer-h3-6-audit.prompt.md:10:- Model: gpt-6.1-sol | Assigned: 2026-11-06 15:17:05 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: Reassigned to Codex (gpt-6.1-sol) on Jupiter to complete the independent H3-6 audit per team.md.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md docs/agents/team.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Lead Engineering Orchestrator role definition for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# ROLE: Lead Engineering Orchestrator\\n\\n## Domain Scope\\n\\n- Task scope, authorization boundaries, and phase completion criteria\\n- Complete ownership of task routing and role assignments as defined in [`team.md`](../team.md)\\n- Timestamped file-based handoffs, the append-only work log, session reuse,\\n  and delegated-agent patience\\n- Independent verification, finding reconciliation, and consensus\\n- Verification and commit-readiness reporting\\n\\nThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts under\\ncoordination and the authority granted to every participant.\\n\\n## Scope of judgment\\n\\nThis role is a secretary, not a decision-maker: it tracks status, routes\\nwork, runs mechanical verification, and makes simple, reversible decisions —\\nnever complex judgment calls, which are always escalated to an Architect\\ndelegate (design/architectural questions) or the user (authorization, scope,\\npriority questions). This scoping is what makes the role viable on a small\\nor fast model: a flash-tier model can reliably do the secretarial half of\\nthis job, but cannot reliably weigh architectural tradeoffs or judge how far\\nan ambiguous instruction reaches.\\n\\nConcretely:\\n\\n- **Simple, orchestrator-owned decisions:** which delegate to route a task to\\n  (per `team.md`'s routing rules), whether local verification passed, whether\\n  a reviewer's finding is already reconciled, file/artifact bookkeeping,\\n  whether to retry a quiet-but-healthy delegate, and implementing small,\\n  low-risk changes directly per Workflow step 4 (e.g. a typo fix, a\\n  mechanical rename, a doc cross-reference update) rather than delegating\\n  them — \\\"simple\\\" here means no choice between competing approaches and no\\n  effect on behavior beyond the literal request.\\n- **Complex, always escalated decisions:** any architectural or design\\n  tradeoff, or any implementation choice that involves picking between\\n  competing approaches rather than a single obvious mechanical change (route\\n  to an Architect delegate, don't reason it out yourself); whether an\\n  ambiguous instruction authorizes a specific consequential action it didn't\\n  state explicitly — e.g. does \\\"make the edit\\\" also authorize committing it,\\n  does \\\"review this\\\" also authorize implementing the fix (ask the user,\\n  don't infer); weighing a reviewer's finding against the design's own\\n  intent when they conflict (route back to the Architect or the user, don't\\n  adjudicate it); any call that would change scope, authorization\\n  boundaries, or what \\\"done\\\" means for the current task (ask the user).\\n- When genuinely unsure which bucket a decision falls in, treat it as\\n  complex and escalate — the cost of an unnecessary question is far lower\\n  than the cost of an orchestrator-level model making an architectural or\\n  authorization call it wasn't equipped to make.\\n\\n## Coordination contract\\n\\nThe orchestrator owns scope, authorization, verification, consensus, and\\nreadiness; the Workflow section owns the sequence.\\n\\n**Commit authority** (2026-10-26 owner ruling). Staging, committing, merging\\nto master, and pushing are authorized by exactly one of:\\n\\n- **An Architect sign-off** ([`architect.md`](architect.md)) on completed\\n  work whose step 7 review has finished and whose findings are reconciled.\\n  The sign-off covers exactly the signed-off work, never unrelated changes\\n  that happen to be in the tree.\\n- **An explicit user instruction.** Only the user can waive step 7 review\\n  (2026-09-23 owner ruling).\\n- **The trivial-edit exemption.** A trivial direct edit as defined in Scope\\n  of judgment (a typo fix, a mechanical rename, a doc cross-reference\\n  update: no choice between approaches, no behavior change) needs neither\\n  review nor sign-off.\\n\\nLocal verification (step 6) is never a substitute for any of these. History\\nrewrites (amend, revert, reset) are outside all three, with one exception: an\\nunauthorized pre-review commit that is local, unpushed, and unmerged is\\nsoft-reset and the work goes through review, never reviewed after the fact.\\nAny other rewrite needs the user. That includes pushed work: a commit that\\nhas reached origin and then fails verification is reported to the user\\nimmediately with the evidence, and only the user authorizes its revert,\\nforward-fix, or force-push — the orchestrator never rewrites pushed history\\non its own authority.\\n\\nFor cost constraints and CLI routing caveats, see [`team.md`](../team.md)'s\\n**Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints** table. For current, session-\\nindependent owner-relayed availability and budget status (an outage, a\\nnarrowed authorization, a near-empty quota), see\\n[`routing-status.md`](../routing-status.md) — check it before routing to a\\nmetered destination, and add an entry when the owner relays a new status.\\n\\n**Delegate dispatches run locally by default (2026-11-03 owner ruling).**\\nA delegate CLI is invoked on THIS machine — the laptop — unless the work\\nitself must run elsewhere: test/nightly lanes on the remote hosts, or a\\nCLI that is genuinely missing locally (checked with `which <cli>` /\\n`<cli> models` first, never assumed). Never route a dispatch to a remote\\nhost because a previous round ran there, because the per-host model\\ncatalog was measured there, or for convenience: per-host catalogs are a\\nreason to run `<cli> models` wherever you dispatch, including locally,\\nbefore writing a model name into a brief. Remote dispatch adds scp and\\nssh round-trips to every turn and resume of the session, with no benefit\\nwhen the local checkout is current (push master first and it always is).\\nRemote dispatch adds scp and\\nssh round-trips to every turn and resume of the session, with no benefit\\nwhen the local checkout is current (push master first and it always is).\\n\\n**Long-running dispatches and remote gates wait via tracked background\\ntasks — never foreground sleeps.** Launch the delegate or the remote\\ngate so the host's task tracking owns the wait: in ZCode, run the\\ncommand with the harness's background mode (`run_in_background`), not\\ndetached via `nohup … &` inside a foreground call — a detached process\\nis invisible to the harness (no UI entry, no completion notification),\\nwhich is how dispatches went missing from the user's view (2026-11-04).\\nRemote gates launched through `bin/collab-tools/rverify.sh` run detached\\nin tmux on the host by design; wait for them with a background task that\\npolls the rverify `poll` subcommand on a long interval (≥ 5 min) and\\nexits when the DONE marker lands — one completion notification, no\\nforeground sleep chains. A foreground `sleep N; poll` chain blocks the\\nturn, risks the harness's command timeout killing the wait mid-poll,\\nand burns turns. If a foreground wait is cancelled or times out, the\\nremote run is NOT affected (tmux survives) — re-check on demand, never\\nrestart it. Report dispatch and completion as explicit status entries so\\nthe user sees every long-running task in the UI.\\n\\n## Artifact protocol\\n\\nCoordination is through repository artifacts, not hidden context. Artifacts\\nare named `<timestamp>-<role>-<task>.prompt.md`,\\n`<timestamp>-<role>-<task>.<sanitized-model>.findings.md`, and `<timestamp>-<role>-<task>.<sanitized-model>.stdout.log`.\\nThe `<timestamp>` prefix is a UNIX millisecond timestamp (e.g. `1725791234567`) so files sort\\nchronologically in Git's untracked view; `<sanitized-model>` prevents parallel reviewers from colliding.\\n\\n**Decide the topic before writing.** An artifact\\nis written directly into its destination topic subfolder\\n(`collab/yang-python/`, `collab/dev/`, … per step 11's folder rules) when\\nthe subsystem it belongs to is known — which is essentially always, since\\ndispatches are issued per track. The flat `collab/` root is reserved for\\nartifacts whose track is genuinely undetermined (cross-track orchestration\\nrulings, coordination whose destination is being decided), and filing them\\nis an explicit orchestrator decision, not a cleanup afterthought. The\\nmotivating incident: the 2026-11-03 worktree-deletion audit\\n(`orchestrator-logs/1793358019`) found that the only work lost when the\\n`-orch` worktree was deleted was unfiled flat-root collab logs, while a\\nfindings file written into `collab/jolt/` from day one survived. Unfiled\\nflat-root files are the fragile state; filed topic files are the durable\\none. In-flight vs archived is carried by GIT, not by the directory:\\nuntracked files (wherever they sit) are in-flight, committed files are the\\nrecord.\\n\\nEvery prompt starts with:\\n\\n```text\\nCreated-GMT: <actual timestamp>\\nCreated-Local: <actual timestamp and named timezone>\\nCoding-Agent: <claude|claude-cloud (claude-api lane)|codex|agy|glm|cmd|muse|deepseek|kimi|interactive>\\nSession-ID: <exact caller UUID | pending (provider-generated) | not-applicable (interactive seat)>\\n# Task: <name>\\nRole: <constant role>\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: <model> | Assigned: <timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\\n```\\n\\nEvery report starts with the same header fields. Use actual timestamps; never fabricate them.\\n\\n`collab/` is append-only: artifacts are never edited after their round\\ncompletes, and `collab/` must never be added to `.gitignore` or\\n`.git/info/exclude` so its files stay visible and chronologically sorted in the user's Magit untracked\\nview. Artifacts stay untracked while in flight — whether they sit in the\\nflat root (undetermined track) or in their topic subfolder (the normal\\ncase, since the filing decision happens at write time) — and become the\\nrecord only through the archive step (Workflow step 11), which commits\\nthem. The two states live in\\ndifferent worlds: the committed topic subfolders are on the master\\nbranch (pushed to origin — master's collab/ subfolders are the durable,\\ndurable-per-seat-readable record), while an in-flight artifact exists\\nonly as an untracked file in the checkout that created it — invisible\\nfrom other checkouts and from every branch until it is committed. This\\nvisibility split is why a merged worktree's collab files must be\\ncommitted before that worktree is deleted (the worktree-deletion rule in\\nthe Worktree section below), and why in-flight artifacts belong in their\\ntopic subfolder from\\nbirth: a committed topic file survives checkout deletion, a flat-root\\nfile does not. Promote final responses to\\n`.findings.md`; `.stdout.log` is only\\nan intermediate capture.\\n\\nOn reassignment, append history inside `Implementers:`; never rewrite an earlier `Status:` line:\\n\\n```text\\n- Status-Event: <timestamp> | Model: <prior-model> | Status: <failed|timed-out|reassigned|superseded> | Rationale: <why>\\n- Model: <new-model> | Assigned: <timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\\n```\\n\\nUse `superseded` when a dispatch is killed or abandoned mid-flight because a new\\ninstruction changed what should be done, not because the delegate failed — this is a\\ndistinct case from `failed`/`timed-out` and should read that way in the artifact trail,\\nnot be inferred from a missing final report.\\n\\nRead-only reviewers may share the main tree. Concurrent editors use separate\\nworktrees from a committed base; uncommitted bases require serialization or\\nexplicit disjoint ownership (lane-sharing limits like \\\"only one process owns\\nthe CLJD lane\\\" are test-execution constraints owned by\\n[`build-n-test.md`](../build-n-test.md), not artifact rules). Auxiliary\\nworktrees lack\\n`collab/`, so briefs use absolute paths, and delegates there often stage\\ntheir own reference copies of prompts/findings in a local `collab/`. The\\ncanonical procedure makes copy-out unnecessary: collab files generated in a\\nworktree are committed there (step 11's `docs(collab):` commit) and reach\\nmaster through the merge. Before removing a worktree, diff its `collab/`\\nagainst the main tree's only as a safety check that no untracked file is\\nbeing lost — a worktree merge moves only committed content. A worktree\\nis deleted only after its work has been successfully merged to master\\nunder that authority — or on user instruction, which is a deliberate\\ndiscard. Because deletion follows the merge, the merged content is\\nalready canonical; confirm only that no other uncommitted file in the\\nworktree is being lost (a closed round has none: its artifacts were\\ncommitted at round-close). Never run `git checkout --` or `git clean`\\non a worktree just to make it removable: that destroys in-flight work\\n(observed 2026-10-26, when P3 oracle fixtures were lost this way).\\n\\nBatch complete briefs, reuse sessions for related follow-ups, start new sessions for unrelated work, and never rely on CLI `--last` flags. Session-ID generation, capture, and resume rules for\\nevery delegate CLI live in\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md#session-continuity),\\nnot here.\\n\\n## Work log\\n\\nEvery orchestrator session starts its own log at session start. Create it at\\nthe repository root as\\n`orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<coding-agent>-<model>-<brief-description>.md`,\\nusing the actual Unix timestamp (`date +%s`) and a short lowercase,\\nhyphen-separated description. Each orchestration is independent: never append\\nto `docs/orchestrator-log.md` or to another session's file, even when the new\\nsession continues an earlier task. `docs/orchestrator-log.md` is the frozen\\nlegacy record from before per-session logs existed — readable as historical\\nclaims only, never extended. When continuing a task, reference the prior\\nsession's log path in the new log's first entry and final handoff instead of\\nsharing or copying the file.\\n\\nThe current session's log is the seat's durable memory. Three entry kinds\\nonly: one per coherent unit (a delegated round, a fix, a review reconciliation,\\na readiness report), an unfinished-work entry when work stops before the next\\nunit finishes, and a final handoff entry when the seat is passed on. It records\\ncompleted and stopped units only; in-flight state lives in `collab/`,\\nreferenced by filename and session ID rather than duplicated. The\\nunfinished-work entry uses the same header schema, with the fields filled\\nfor what IS true: `Completed-GMT` timestamps the stop, `Done` names exactly\\nwhat landed and what did not, and the body ends with the remaining steps so\\na successor can continue from the artifact trail alone.\\n\\nStart every entry with actual timestamps and identity fields (never fabricate\\nthem), then state what `git log` alone cannot re-derive:\\n\\n```text\\n## <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name> — <task name>\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCoding-Agent: <one of the values in the prompt header above>\\nSession-ID: <exact Session-ID | not-applicable (interactive seat)>\\nTree: <branch>@<short-sha>, <committed | uncommitted changes: <files>>\\nDone: <what changed and why, with paths>\\nDecisions: <choices made, alternatives rejected, and their reasons>\\nVerification: <exact commands with outcomes and assertion counts; name unrun checks>\\nDelegates: <role/model, prompt and findings filenames, session IDs> | none\\nNext: <the next coherent unit, open risks, blockers>\\n```\\n\\nWithin its owning session, the log is append-only: never edit, reorder, or\\ndelete an earlier entry — a correction is a new entry naming what it corrects.\\nWhen an entry names a `collab/` artifact, repeat its session ID so a successor\\ncan resume that session under the continuity rules above. The log may describe\\nwork that was never committed or has since been superseded: the tree is\\ncanonical, so a successor creates its own log and still re-derives state from\\n`git log`, `git status`, and the real diff, treating every prior log entry as a\\nclaim to verify rather than authority.\\n\\n## Authorization and security\\n\\nPrivate repository content may be sent externally only with explicit user\\nauthorization for the exact payload and destination. Invoke from the authorized\\nagent; consent does not carry over. Pass prompt paths or stdin, never private\\ndiffs or credentials in arguments. Use read-only/plan review and minimum write\\nscope; never bypass permission checks. Preserve unrelated changes and never\\nexpose or commit tokens or configuration. The same rule binds delegates: a\\ndelegate that requests a credential gets the requirement escalated to the\\nuser — never satisfied by pasting a secret into a prompt file or environment\\nthe orchestrator controls. Before an archive commit, scan the batch for\\ncredential-shaped strings (keys, tokens, `sk-`, bearer values); a stdout log\\nthat captured a secret is redacted or left uncommitted and the incident\\nrecorded in the work log.\\n\\n## Workflow\\n\\nThis section owns the execution sequence. Roster, role routing, and reviewer\\nindependence are defined in [`team.md`](../team.md); CLI flags and recipes are\\ndefined in [`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md);\\ncommit message format is defined in [`format.md`](../format.md#git-commit-messages).\\n\\n1. **Establish the seat.** The harness must be able to read and write the working\\n   tree, run every affected host's checks, and invoke delegate CLIs. Establish\\n   those capabilities before accepting work. At session start, create this\\n   session's own `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<coding-agent>-<model>-<brief-description>.md`\\n   file under the repository root as specified in Work log. If a required\\n   capability is missing, report it and do not issue a blind sign-off.\\n2. **Re-derive state and authority.** Read `git log`, `git status`, and the real\\n   diff; snapshots and phase summaries may be stale. Read\\n   [`docs/agents/routing-status.md`](../routing-status.md) for current\\n   owner-relayed delegate availability and budget constraints. When explicitly\\n   continuing an earlier orchestration, read its referenced prior log as\\n   historical claims, never as a file to append to. Bound the user's authorized\\n   files, tools, payloads, and external destinations.\\n3. **Define the contract.** Express the task as tests or equally precise\\n   acceptance criteria, invariants, phase-completion criteria, and bounded file\\n   ownership.\\n4. **Choose execution and review routes.** Follow the role selection and reviewer independence rules in\\n   [`team.md`](../team.md). Implement simple, low-risk work directly when delegation would cost more in\\n   coordination, tokens, or review. Otherwise, assign a specialized implementation role to the optimal\\n   model per `team.md`'s model tables; its cost-constraint table takes precedence over model fit. Add\\n   Architect or Security review when the role or risk requires it.\\n5. **Brief and execute.** When delegating, use the selected role's delegation\\n   prompt template — [`team.md`](../team.md)'s Roster table links each role\\n   name to its template file under `docs/agents/roles/`; do not improvise a\\n   brief from the roster's one-line description alone. Keep the brief\\n   concise and unambiguous. When it relays an owner\\n   instruction that is being reinterpreted, corrected, or applied to a design\\n   (not simply passed through verbatim), quote the owner's exact words and\\n   label them as a quote, kept visibly separate from your own paraphrase of\\n   what they mean — a paraphrase fused into one voice hides the gap where a\\n   misreading survives to execution instead of being caught first (this\\n   happened in this project's own history: an ambiguous \\\"no legacy support\\\"\\n   instruction was paraphrased as \\\"drop the old algorithm entirely,\\\" the\\n   opposite of what was meant, and nearly reached a delegate before the owner\\n   caught it). If a plausible alternate reading of the owner's words exists,\\n   ask before dispatching rather than letting a delegate execute an\\n   unconfirmed interpretation. A delegate's exit code or promise is not a\\n   deliverable: inspect the artifact and resume an unfinished turn. Quiet\\n   output is not failure; wait for completion or an explicit error. Do not\\n   terminate a healthy agent merely because it is quiet for several minutes\\n   (especially true for DeepSeek).\\n   - **Quiet vs. stalled is a procedure, not a judgment.** A delegate is\\n     stalled only when, for 15 minutes, its stdout log has not grown AND no\\n     live process matches it (`pgrep -f <cli>`); check the CLI's quirk entry\\n     in [`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md)\\n     before concluding (some quiet runs are healthy, some \\\"session in use\\\"\\n     messages mean the first run is still alive). A stalled run is killed,\\n     its artifact inspected for partial work, and the round recovered by\\n     resume-by-state or fresh session — never silently re-dispatched as if\\n     nothing happened.\\n   - **Delegate death has a terminal state.** After two failed rounds on the\\n     same unit (different sessions, and at least two different models where\\n     the roster allows), stop dispatching: record both failures in the work\\n     log with their artifact trails and escalate to the user with the\\n     evidence. A third model is chosen only on user instruction — an\\n     unbounded retry loop is the failure mode this rule exists to end.\\n6. **Verify — individual tests may run locally; suites run remotely (owner\\n   ruling 2026-11-04).** Inspect the actual artifact and diff in the\\n   orchestrator's environment. An individual test is fast: a single failing\\n   test or one namespace's reproduction (`-v <ns>/<var>`, `-n <ns>`,\\n   `--test=<ns>`, `--only <ns>`) may run on the laptop. Everything\\n   suite-scale — subsystem lane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), full\\n   suites, the slow tier, the nightly — runs on the remote hosts\\n   (shiva/jupiter): the suite has outgrown the laptop, and local suite runs\\n   are too slow and load the machine the owner is using. Delegate agents\\n   (`claude`, `codex`, `glm`, …) can execute on the remote test hosts\\n   directly — see [`build-n-test.md`](../build-n-test.md), section \\\"Remote\\n   agent execution\\\" (the laptop-unload rule: bookkeeping locally, execution\\n   remotely) and \\\"Remote test servers\\\" for the host recipes (scratch clones\\n   under `~/ci/`, mise activation order, per-host lane overlap limits).\\n   Delegated test claims are untrusted: the orchestrator reads the\\n   remote run's actual output (log paths on the host), not the delegate's\\n   summary. User-run results count as evidence only when the exact command,\\n   output, and tested revision are available; otherwise run the checks.\\n   Tell later reviewers which checks already passed so they spend their\\n   budget on static analysis instead of redundant suites, except when\\n   security review requires a rerun.\\n7. **Review and reconcile.** Give the verified diff to an independent reviewer\\n   before committing (exceptions: the coordination contract's user waiver and\\n   trivial-edit exemption). Weigh findings on their merits, fix accepted\\n   defects, and resume the same reviewer to confirm the correction. Preserve\\n   every round. Findings are reconciled when every one carries a recorded\\n   disposition: fixed and reviewer-confirmed, rejected with evidence, or\\n   deferred with user approval. A reviewer–architect dispute is terminated by\\n   one architect adjudication per unit: the ruling is final for that unit,\\n   and residual disagreement is recorded as an unresolved risk in the step 8\\n   readiness report — never re-litigated by re-dispatching review rounds.\\n8. **Report readiness.** Report exact commands, assertion counts, reviewer\\n   sign-off, unrun checks, unreviewed changes, unresolved risks, and any tool or\\n   hook noise. Do not let the summary outrun the evidence. Append the unit's\\n   entry to this session's own file under `orchestrator-logs/` in the same terms.\\n9. **Stage, commit, and merge only when authorized** under the coordination\\n   contract's commit authority. Stage only the authorized work, inspect the\\n   staged diff immediately before committing, and commit only that diff.\\n   Use the commit message format in\\n   [`format.md`](../format.md#git-commit-messages). Before staging in the\\n   shared main tree, re-run `git status` and compare against the state\\n   derived in step 2: any working-tree change not attributable to this\\n   seat's units (another seat's or the user's in-flight work) freezes the\\n   commit — escalate, never stage around it, and never `git add -A` in the\\n   main tree.\\n10. **Verify what landed.** Compare the commit's diff with the authorized\\n    staged diff. If hooks or formatters changed what landed, review that delta and\\n    rerun its affected checks — the pre-commit formatter hook can stage files\\n    you never added (observed 2026-10-26: an untracked in-flight file of\\n    another track was swept into a ledger commit; caught only by reading the\\n    post-commit `--stat` against intent). A failure means the work is not\\n    ready; rewrite history only as the coordination contract allows.\\n    **Shared append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`) have a merge\\n    rule** (2026-10-26, after merge 4d6fd818 silently dropped ~66 rows by\\n    replacing the file with a rewritten 16-row variant): never resolve a\\n    ledger conflict by taking one side's file, and never land a rewritten or\\n    reformatted ledger inside a feature merge. The ledger is append-only\\n    ROW-WISE: the only correct resolution is the union of both sides' rows\\n    under the existing column schema; a schema change is a proposal to the\\n    work log, agreed by seats, executed as a row-wise migration. Before\\n    pushing any commit or merge that touches the ledger, count rows on both\\n    parents and on the result — the result must be at least max(parents):\\n    `git show <rev>:docs/agents/known-gaps.md | grep -c '^|'`. A smaller\\n    number means you just dropped another seat's rows; restore per the union\\n    rule before pushing.\\n11. **Archive completed-task artifacts.** When collaboration artifacts are no\\n    longer needed for active collaboration (their task is committed or its\\n    track is otherwise closed), commit them — the storage model, the\\n    write-time filing rule, and the ruling history are canonical in the\\n    Artifact protocol; this step is only the mechanical procedure.\\n    Artifacts filed at write time are archived by `git add collab/<topic>/`\\n    plus the commit; the only files that need MOVING are flat-root\\n    stragglers whose track was undetermined at write time — move those into\\n    git-tracked, subsystem-organized subfolders of the same directory (e.g.\\n    `collab/yang-python/`, `collab/dao-stream/`, `collab/yin-ucf/`,\\n    `collab/yin-linker/`) under their exact filenames. Every archive batch\\n    is committed (and pushed) as `docs(archive):` or `docs(collab):`; an\\n    archive that is not committed is not an archive. Do not leave archived\\n    files untracked, and do not invent intermediate locations. Organize\\n    archived files by design subsystem, not by generic slice codes or\\n    temporary folder names:\\n\\n    - **The subsystem is defined by the code, not by the seat.** When the work\\n      belongs to a subsystem in [`src/dev/subsystems.edn`](../../../src/dev/subsystems.edn)\\n      (the same registry `bb test:sub` uses), the destination folder is that\\n      subsystem's name with dots replaced by dashes (`yang.python` ->\\n      `collab/yang-python/`, `yin.vm` -> `collab/yin-vm/`). Do not invent a\\n      variant spelling of a subsystem that exists.\\n    - **Check before creating.** Before creating any new destination folder,\\n      list `collab/`'s existing subfolders and reuse one when the work belongs\\n      there — including cross-cutting categories that predate the subsystem\\n      registry (`collab/orchestration/`, `collab/general/`, and track\\n      categories like `collab/dao-star/`, `collab/jolt/`). A new folder is\\n      created only when no existing subfolder fits; name it after the\\n      subsystem when there is one, otherwise after the durable track or topic\\n      (never a date, a slice code, or a seat name). When neither the registry\\n      nor an existing folder fits, propose the new folder name in the step 8\\n      readiness report for user or architect confirmation instead of creating\\n      it unilaterally — folder names are durable, and naming is a shared\\n      convention, not a per-seat choice.\\n    - **One artifact, one destination.** Same-named collisions resolve by\\n      content comparison, never by size or ending heuristics: identical bytes\\n      drop the duplicate; differing same-name artifacts are kept as suffixed\\n      variants (the `-b`, `-r2` convention) — neither copy dies.\\n\\n    Never delete, truncate, rename, or overwrite an artifact. Same-named\\n    collisions (in whatever folder they meet) resolve by the rule above;\\n    a `mv` that would land on an existing file leaves the collision in\\n    place for that resolution. Archive only committed work and only when\\n    no uncommitted tracked changes could make task ownership ambiguous.\\n\\nIf the remaining budget cannot finish the next coherent unit, leave the tree\\nreadable and record incomplete work in the findings rather than leaving a\\nhalf-applied edit, and append the unfinished-work entry naming what remains\\nso a successor can continue it. The trigger is explicit: when the seat\\nestimates its remaining budget is less than one more coherent unit requires,\\nit writes the unfinished-work entry and issues the Seat Handoff Template\\n(below) before attempting further work — it does not start a unit it cannot\\nfinish.\\n\\n**Blocked on the user: park, don't poll.** When progress requires a user\\nanswer (authorization, scope, an adjudication this doc reserves to the\\nuser), stop the work: park every delegate (no session left running or\\nmetered while idle — the 144-minute idle-session incident is the cost this\\nrule exists to end), record the blocker and the pending question in the\\nwork log's unfinished-work entry, and end the turn. The seat resumes only\\nwhen the user's answer arrives.\\n\\n## Orchestrator Seat Handoff Template\\n\\nUse this template only when handing the entire orchestrator seat to another\\nagent. Implementation and review delegates use their own role templates; they\\nmust not select or invoke further agents unless the user's authorization and\\ntheir brief explicitly grant that coordination role.\\n\\n```text\\nCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\nCoding-Agent: <coding-agent-or-cli>\\nSession-ID: <caller-generated UUID | pending (provider-generated) | not-applicable (interactive seat)>\\n\\n# Task: <Task Name>\\n\\nRole: Lead Engineering Orchestrator\\n\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone> | Status: active | Rationale: Initial assignment\\n\\nCoordinate <phase-or-task> in <repository-root>.\\n\\nRead first:\\n- <governing-design-file>\\n- <current-phase-status>\\n- <relevant-source-and-test-files>\\n- <prior-orchestrator-log-path-if-explicit-continuation> (historical claims only; do not append)\\n- `docs/agents/routing-status.md` (current owner-relayed delegate availability and budget status)\\n- `docs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md` (session-ID mechanics, CLI flags, and recipes)\\n\\nEvery claim in this brief, in any prior orchestrator log, and in\\nrouting-status.md is something the outgoing seat believed at handoff time, not\\nverified fact — re-derive tree state yourself from `git log`, `git status`, and\\nthe real diff before acting on any of it, the same posture this role's own Work\\nLog section requires of any successor.\\n\\nRequired workflow:\\n- Follow `docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md#workflow` in order.\\n- Create a new session log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<coding-agent>-<model>-<brief-description>.md`; never append to the prior session's log.\\n- Run <focused-test-command> on the remote test hosts (shiva/jupiter) per Workflow step 6; <lint-command> may run locally.\\n\\nDo not broaden scope; do not stage, commit, or merge without an Architect\\nsign-off, an explicit user instruction, or the trivial-edit exemption\\n(see the Coordination contract above); do not trust\\ndelegated test claims without local evidence, or terminate a healthy agent\\nmerely because it is slow or temporarily quiet.\\n\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\nCoding-Agent: <same coding agent used for the run>\\nSession-ID: <exact initial Session-ID value, with provider-generated value promoted after capture>\\n\\nThen report delegated roles/models, prompts and session IDs, verified findings,\\ntest outcomes, unresolved risks, and whether the phase is ready to commit.\\nAppend the final handoff entry to this session's new log before responding,\\nreferencing the prior session's log path when continuing earlier work.\\n```\\n\\n## Delegate Invocation Reference\\n\\nCLI flags, canonical recipes, and known quirks for every delegate CLI\\n(claude, claude-cloud [the claude-api lane wrapper], glm, deepseek, muse, codex, cmd, agy, kimi) live in\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md),\\nnot here — read it before shelling out to any delegate CLI. It is reference\\nmaterial needed at the moment of dispatch, not on every orchestrator turn,\\nwhich is why it is kept separate from this role's core identity and workflow.\\nDispatch is always by direct CLI invocation per that reference's canonical\\nrecipes. The legacy `dispatch.sh` helper has been deleted.\\n---\\ndescription: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD SOFTWARE ENGINEERING TEAM\\n\\nCanonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\\nThe Lead Engineering Orchestrator owns coordination, authorization, artifact\\nprotocol, and verification in [`orchestrator.md`](./roles/orchestrator.md);\\ndelegate session protocol and CLI recipes live in\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](./delegate-invocation-reference.md).\\n\\n## Roster\\n\\nThe Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table; it is the user-facing\\nagent that centrally routes tasks to these roles. Each role name links to its\\ndelegation prompt template under `docs/agents/roles/`. Review is one role\\nbacked by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\\nsecurity-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\\ntemplates — adjust scope and rigor in the brief per flavor, not the file.\\n\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Role                                              | Responsibility                                                                                         |\\n+===================================================+========================================================================================================+\\n| [Architect](roles/architect.md)                   | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                                                           |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Compiler & AST](roles/compiler-engineer.md)      | AST, lowering, compile-time macros                                                                     |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Frontend & Graphics](roles/graphics-engineer.md) | Events, WebGL/WebGPU, canvas, terminal                                                                 |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [QA & Verification](roles/qa-engineer.md)         | TDD, parity, lint, regression                                                                          |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Scoped / Subagent](roles/subagent.md)            | Bounded searches, edits, docs, lint                                                                    |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Storage & Indexing](roles/storage-engineer.md)   | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                                                                 |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [Stream & Network](roles/stream-engineer.md)      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC                                                 |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| [VM Runtime](roles/vm-engineer.md)                | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                                                                |\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\n\\n## Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints\\n\\nThe Orchestrator routes work using these cost profiles. Flat subscriptions are preferred; metered models are reserved for tasks explicitly requiring them.\\n\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Provider        | Cost Structure                         | Routing Notes                                                                                               |\\n+=================+========================================+=============================================================================================================+\\n| agy             | Flat: Included                         | Treated as a flat subscription. Use freely within budget.                                                   |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| qwen            | Token Plan subscription (Alibaba Cloud | The `qwen` CLI is Alibaba's own coding agent (`@qwen-code/qwen-code@0.17.0`, npm-global under nvm node      |\\n|                 | Model Studio, ap-southeast-1)          | v26.1.0), not a Claude Code wrapper. Headless dispatch and session mechanics measured (2026-11-04): caller  |\\n|                 |                                        | `--session-id <uuid>`, follow-up `qwen --resume <uuid>` (round-trip verified; chats stored per project      |\\n|                 |                                        | under ~/.qwen/projects/). Implementation requires `--approval-mode auto-edit`: the default mode silently    |\\n|                 |                                        | no-ops tools headless. Config `~/.qwen/settings.json`: key in env `BAILIAN_TOKEN_PLAN_API_KEY`; gateway     |\\n|                 |                                        | https://token-plan.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1 (the key 403s at cn-beijing).        |\\n|                 |                                        | Measured catalog: `qwen3.8-max` (default), `qwen3.8-flash`, `qwen3.7-max`, `qwen3.6-flash`,                 |\\n|                 |                                        | `deepseek-v4-pro`; all others denied or absent. Routing status: available NOW (coding round-trip passed:    |\\n|                 |                                        | files written, assertions passed, resume verified).                                                         |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| claude          | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Subscription stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                                     |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| claude-api      | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Anthropic API access stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                             |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Available for any role (architecture, implementation, review, QA).                      |\\n|                 |                                        | All codex models (astra, sol, terra, luna) draw ONE shared pool: a cut darkens every seat at once, so       |\\n|                 |                                        | sequence codex dispatches rather than fanning them out. Independent parallel capacity is codex | kimi | glm.|\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| deepseek        | Metered: Pay-by-token                  | Flash: $0.14/M in, $0.28/M out. Pro: $0.435/M in, $0.87/M out.                                              |\\n|                 |                                        | Off-peak (10:00-01:00, 04:00-06:00 UTC & wknds) is 50% cheaper.                                             |\\n|                 |                                        | Cache hits are ~50x cheaper. Reserve for necessary work.                                                    |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| glm             | Flat: Pro yearly plan ($672/yr)        | Peak hours: weekdays 14:00-18:00 UTC+8. Schedule large jobs off-peak (50% rate).                            |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| kimi            | Flat: Kimi membership (Plus+)          | Invoke only through the dedicated `kimi` CLI. Monthly quotas apply.                                         |\\n|                 |                                        | Pro unlocks Highspeed and 1M context for K3.x.                                                              |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| mistral         | Flat: Mistral Pro ($20/mo)             | Invoke via `vibe` CLI. API-live aliases (probed 2026-11-04): `mistral-4.0` (the current large-4 generation; |\\n|                 |                                        | use it for large-model work) and `mistral-medium-3.5`. The bare `mistral-large` alias is DEAD at the API:   |\\n|                 |                                        | the CLI parses it but the live API answers 400 invalid_model (same for `devstral` and `codestral`);         |\\n|                 |                                        | anything else (`mistral-small`, `magistral-*`, `mixtral-*`) is not even CLI-recognized.                     |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| muse            | Meta login / `META_API_KEY`            | Invoke via `muse` CLI; headless form is `muse exec`. Models:                                                |\\n|                 |                                        | `muse-wonder-1.0` (flagship) and `muse-spark-1.3` (fast lane).                                              |\\n|                 |                                        | Check [`routing-status.md`](./routing-status.md) before dispatch (geo-restricted at last relay).            |\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n\\n## Model Strengths and Selection Guide\\n\\nThe following guide details the strengths, weaknesses, and optimal use cases for each model based on their current (late 2026) technical capabilities.\\n\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| Model                       | Strengths & When to Use                                            | Weaknesses & When Not to Use                                        |\\n+=============================+====================================================================+=====================================================================+\\n| `claude-fable-5-1`          | Highest capability for long-horizon agentic work, complex research,| Slower inference; resource hog (rate limits); mandatory             |\\n|                             | strict boundaries, and managing vast context (efficient caching).  | watermarking. **Avoid for:** Routine fast-loop tasks; precise       |\\n|                             | **Best for:** Architecture definition, security sign-offs.         | one-shot implementations where strict rigor is overkill.            |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `claude-opus-5.5`           | Performs at Fable 5.1 levels for most work at markedly lower cost  | Still higher latency/cost than Sonnet 5. **Avoid for:** Fast QA     |\\n|                             | than the prior Opus generation. **Best for:** Code migration,      | loops or simple code generation where Sonnet 5 suffices.            |\\n|                             | software optimization, and complex agentic coding.                 |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `claude-sonnet-5-5`         | Frontier agentic workhorse; substantial gains in reasoning depth,  | Can struggle with high-order axiomatic proofs or subtle security    |\\n|                             | instruction following, and rigorous code audits. Low latency and   | capability proofs vs Fable 5.1 / Astra. **Avoid for:** Final        |\\n|                             | high cost-efficiency. **Best for:** Core implementation, deep QA   | architectural sign-offs on high-risk cryptographic or consensus     |\\n|                             | adversarial code reviews, multi-file refactors, and test suites.   | boundaries where Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1 / Astra is mandated.          |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `deepseek-flash`            | V4.1-Flash outpaces V4-Pro in speed, cost, and agentic benchmarks. | Can hallucinate on deep invariant constraints. **Avoid for:**       |\\n|                             | **Best for:** QA parity checks, scoped subagent work, and          | Complex system design, AST lowering.                                |\\n|                             | high-efficiency native multimodal tasks.                           |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `deepseek-v4-pro`           | Legacy 1.6T MoE architecture. **Best for:** Advanced logic         | Actively being superseded by V4.1-Flash; high cost and undercooked  |\\n|                             | workflows and adversarial reviews.                                 | relative to size. **Avoid for:** General use (use Flash instead).   |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gemini-3.8-flash`          | Lightning-fast; highly cost-efficient; 1M+ context with native     | Can oversimplify subtle invariant edge cases vs frontier models.    |\\n|                             | multimodal support. **Best for:** High-volume agentic loops,       | **Avoid for:** Core security capability enforcement; top-level      |\\n|                             | visual/frontend inspection, rapid TDD/QA, and subagents.           | architecture.                                                       |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gemini-4-argon`            | Frontier reasoning with configurable effort and deep synthesis     | Higher latency/cost at medium/high effort than Flash-tier models;   |\\n|                             | across large multi-file specs and codebases; strong architectural  | sandboxed headless CLI cannot run local JVM (`clojure`) suites.     |\\n|                             | judgment, invariant verification, and surgical cross-checking.     | **Avoid for:** High-volume trivial edits, mechanical sweeps, or     |\\n|                             | **Best for:** System architecture, design/code review, complex     | headless tasks requiring in-session JVM test execution.             |\\n|                             | debugging, and multi-step implementation.                          |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `glm-5.3`                   | Scaled post-training for exceptional long-horizon tasks and        | Heavier footprint; can overcomplicate simple data framing. **Avoid  |\\n|                             | cybersecurity. **Best for:** Complex programming workflows, VMs,   | for:** UI/UX, canvas, or WebGPU tasks.                              |\\n|                             | storage, indexing, DHTs, and networking.                           |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `glm-5.3-flash`             | High performance at a lower computational cost; steep discounts    | Limited complex reasoning on novel architectures; self-assessment   |\\n|                             | off-peak and weekend boosts. Excels when claims are checkable      | is unreliable — judge it behaviorally. **Avoid for:** Core          |\\n|                             | against an authority: evidence gathering, citation verification,   | architecture, unbounded design authorship, peak-hour execution if   |\\n|                             | reconciliation, protocol discipline. **Best for:** Scoped          | budget is tight.                                                    |\\n|                             | subagents, bounded searches, QA spot-checks, verification seats.   |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gpt-5.4-mini`              | Fast, lower-cost model for bounded, repetitive work with explicit  | Do not use as the sole judge of subtle invariants or broad design.  |\\n|                             | acceptance criteria. **Best for:** TDD loops, simple linting,      | Escalate multi-step refactors and disputed findings to Terra, Sol,  |\\n|                             | extraction, and scoped text edits.                                 | or Astra.                                                           |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gpt-6-astra`               | Most capable end-to-end model. **Best for:** Hardest architecture, | Highest cost. Reserve for the highest-risk or most difficult work   |\\n|                             | security, research, coding, computer-use, and long-horizon tasks.  | under the subscription policy.                                      |\\n|                             | Capability includes implementation across any role.                |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gpt-6-luna`                | Most affordable GPT-6 model; optimized for high-volume tasks and   | Low-cost does not mean suitable for unbounded design or the hardest |\\n|                             | fast response times. Improved factuality and coding. **Best for:** | invariant reasoning. Escalate complex architecture/security.        |\\n|                             | High-volume tasks, extraction, scoped searches, and routine QA.    |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gpt-6.1-sol`               | Intelligent everyday workhorse with near-Astra intelligence, 1M+   | More expensive than Luna. Use Astra when the task is the hardest    |\\n|                             | context window, and 95% prompt cache discount. Upgraded reasoning  | end-to-end problem requiring frontier architectural verification.   |\\n|                             | effort control (low to max), +6.4% DeepSWE / +7% OSWorld gains over|                                                                     |\\n|                             | predecessor. **Best for:** Complex implementation, advanced coding,|                                                                     |\\n|                             | high-stakes security, and architectural review.                    |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `gpt-6.1-terra`             | Balanced intelligence and cost. **Best for:** Default production   | Use Sol or Astra for the hardest reasoning, high-risk security, or  |\\n|                             | engineering, integration work, network streams, VM runtimes,       | architecture decisions. Validate compiler-lowering and other        |\\n|                             | moderate-complexity implementation, and subagent fallback.         | specialist work with focused tests and independent review.          |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `kimi-for-coding-highspeed` | K2.7 Code Highspeed; 5-6x faster output with 256K context.         | Uses about 3x quota; fixed thinking; Pro tier required. **Avoid     |\\n|                             | **Best for:** Latency-sensitive routine coding and iteration.      | for:** 1M-context work or tasks needing adjustable reasoning.       |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `k3.1-pro`                  | Latest 3T-class flagship; 1M context, image/video input, and       | Higher resource use than K3 256K; thinking is mandatory; 1M         |\\n|                             | strongest long-horizon agentic coding. **Best for:** Hardest       | context requires Pro. **Avoid for:** Routine Clojure or latency-    |\\n|                             | implementation, large refactors, architecture, deep review, and    | sensitive loops; use K3 256K or Highspeed instead. Also avoid       |\\n|                             | difficult multi-file Clojure, compiler, VM, and invariant work.    | clients that cannot keep thinking enabled.                          |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `k3.1-pro-highspeed`        | Same K3.1 Pro capabilities with 2-3x faster output and 1M          | About 2x quota usage; Pro tier; accelerates model output, not       |\\n|                             | context. **Best for:** Responsive complex coding, long-horizon     | tools. **Avoid for:** Quota-sensitive or tool-bound work where      |\\n|                             | agents, and iterative difficult debugging.                         | regular K3.1 Pro has the same quality at lower quota cost.          |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `k3`                        | Previous 2.8T flagship; 1M context, image/video input, adjustable  | Superseded by K3.1 Pro; 1M uses about 2x the quota of K3 256K       |\\n|                             | thinking. **Best for:** Large-codebase analysis, multi-file        | and requires Pro. **Avoid for:** Hardest tasks when K3.1 Pro is     |\\n|                             | refactors, and long-document processing.                           | available, or small jobs that fit K3 256K.                          |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `k3-256k`                   | Same K3 results within 256K at about half the 1M-context quota.    | 256K limit; image input only, no video. **Avoid for:** Vast         |\\n|                             | Most quota-efficient Clojure choice; use low thinking for routine  | contexts, long documents, video, or difficult multi-file Clojure,   |\\n|                             | REPL fixes, features, and small/medium refactors. **Best for:**    | compiler, VM, and invariant work; use K3.1 Pro for those instead.   |\\n|                             | routine coding, code completion, Q&A, and small edits.             |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `mistral-4.0`               | The current large-4 generation and this lane's API-live large      | Context window not probe-verified from this seat (the reachable     |\\n|                             | alias (probed 2026-11-04). Strong general reasoning and code       | provider docs predate the 4.0 generation); probe before             |\\n|                             | generation. **Best for:** General implementation, refactors, and   | long-document work. **Avoid for:** Deep invariant proofs; use Fable |\\n|                             | code review on the mistral lane.                                   | 5.1/Opus 5.5 on the highest-stakes cryptographic boundaries.        |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `mistral-medium-3.5`        | API-live via `vibe` (probed 2026-11-04); the lane's efficient      | Lighter reasoning than the mistral-4.0 generation (tier             |\\n|                             | mid-tier. **Best for:** Routine coding, QA, extraction, and scoped | positioning; not benchmarked here). **Avoid for:** Complex          |\\n|                             | edits where the mistral family fits.                               | architecture or deep invariant reasoning.                           |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `codestral`                 | Low-latency code specialist: fill-in-the-middle, code correction,  | NOT dispatchable on this lane: the CLI parses `codestral` but the   |\\n|                             | test generation; 256K context per the provider's 25.01 model card  | API answers 400 invalid_model (probed 2026-11-03 and 11-04).        |\\n|                             | (source-derived, not probe-verified).                              | **Avoid for:** all routing until the lane revives it; narrow code   |\\n|                             |                                                                    | focus, not a reviewer.                                              |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `devstral`                  | The family's agentic software-engineering specialist (public       | NOT dispatchable on this lane: parses client-side, API 400          |\\n|                             | positioning; source-derived, not measured here). **Best for:**     | invalid_model (probed 2026-11-03 and 11-04). **Avoid for:** all     |\\n|                             | repo-level agentic coding sweeps if the lane ever revives it.      | routing today.                                                      |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `magistral-medium-latest`   | The family's reasoning-tuned medium line (source-derived, not      | NOT dispatchable via `vibe` at all: not in the CLI alias list       |\\n|                             | measured here). **Best for:** deliberative reasoning chains if a   | (Unknown model, probed 2026-11-04). **Avoid for:** all routing      |\\n|                             | lane ever wires it.                                                | today.                                                              |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `mixtral-8x7b`              | Sparse Mixture of Experts; strong coding at lower cost.            | **Avoid for:** Tasks requiring dense attention across very long     |\\n|                             | **Best for:** Implementation, debugging, and moderate complexity.   | contexts or fine-grained invariant verification.                   |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `muse-wonder-1.0`           | Flagship Muse (Meta) model; the model behind the Muse              | Heavier and slower than Spark. **Avoid for:** High-volume trivial   |\\n|                             | orchestrator seat in this repo: long-horizon agentic coding,       | edits, extraction, or latency-sensitive loops; use                  |\\n|                             | multi-file implementation, and deep cross-checking.                | `muse-spark-1.3` instead.                                           |\\n|                             | **Best for:** Orchestration, complex implementation, deep review.  |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `muse-spark-1.3`            | Fast Muse lane; the auxiliary/helper model inside Muse             | Lighter reasoning than Wonder. **Avoid for:** Hardest architecture, |\\n|                             | sessions. **Best for:** Scoped subagent work, bounded searches,    | security sign-offs, or subtle invariant proofs; escalate to         |\\n|                             | routine QA, extraction, and fast iteration.                        | `muse-wonder-1.0`.                                                  |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `qwen3.8-max`               | Qwen (Alibaba) flagship on the `qwen` CLI's Token Plan (serving    | Capability not benchmarked from this fleet yet (availability        |\\n|                             | model, probed live 2026-11-04); latest generation. **Best for:**   | measured only); treat early dispatches as candidates for the        |\\n|                             | Implementation and review rounds on the qwen lane, pending in-repo | standing kimi quality gate before trusting verdicts. **Avoid for:** |\\n|                             | evaluation.                                                        | Long-context jobs: context size not probe-verified.                 |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `qwen3.8-flash`             | Qwen fast tier on the same plan (probed live 2026-11-04). **Best   | Lighter tier than 3.8-max. **Avoid for:** Hardest reasoning or      |\\n|                             | for:** Latency-sensitive iteration, QA spot-checks, extraction,    | disputed findings; use `qwen3.8-max` or escalate per the ladder.    |\\n|                             | and this seat's own interactive runs.                              |                                                                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `qwen3.7-max`               | Prior-generation Qwen flagship, still served on the plan (probed   | Superseded within the lane by `qwen3.8-max`. **Avoid for:** Any     |\\n|                             | live). Second choice when 3.8 capacity is throttled.               | work 3.8 can take; no measured advantage over it.                   |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `qwen3.6-flash`             | Prior-generation Qwen fast tier (probed live).                     | Two generations old; lighter reasoning than either 3.8 tier.        |\\n|                             |                                                                    | **Avoid for:** Anything the 3.8 lane can serve.                     |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n| `deepseek-v4-pro` (qwen     | NOTE: the `qwen` lane's Token Plan ALSO serves `deepseek-v4-pro`   | Same-family collision: a deepseek-v4-pro author on the qwen lane    |\\n| lane note)                  | (probed live 2026-11-04), in addition to the Qwen tiers.           | must NOT be reviewed by the DeepSeek lane (same family).            |\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n## Role Selection & Reviewer Independence\\n\\nThe Orchestrator is completely responsible for selecting the implementer for a given role. It dynamically\\ncross-references the task's requirements against the **Model Strengths and Selection Guide** and the\\n**Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints**. Any model may fill any role provided its technical profile and\\ncost align with the task.\\n\\n**Reviewer Independence Rules:**\\nEvery change must be reviewed by a model from a **different family** to ensure\\nindependent defect discovery. Never silently substitute a same-family reviewer.\\nThe families represented in the Model Strengths table are: **Claude**,\\n**DeepSeek**, **Gemini**, **GLM**, **GPT**, **Kimi**, **Mistral**,\\n**Muse** (Meta), and **Qwen** (Alibaba). Family, not CLI, decides\\nindependence: the `qwen` lane's `deepseek-v4-pro` is a **DeepSeek**\\nauthor for review purposes, and Qwen-authored work (including anything\\nby this interactive seat) must be reviewed by a different family.\\nAny author from one family must be reviewed by a model from a different family.\\n\\nRoutine review applies only when independent; architectural or security review\\nis mandatory when the role or risk requires it. For operational procedures, see\\nthe [`Lead Engineering Orchestrator`](./roles/orchestrator.md).\\n\\n**Reviewer bench and parallel-lane scheduling** (2026-10-26 owner ruling,\\nafter the jolt lane's throughput review):\\n\\n- **The standing reviewer bench** is wider than the implementation bench:\\n  `kimi` (flat membership, native Kimi models only) joins the families above\\n  as a standing review destination. The independence rule is unchanged: the\\n  reviewer must be a different model family from the author.\\n- **Parallel-lane scheduling**: when slice N lands on its branch, slice N+1's\\n  implementation dispatches immediately and slice N's review runs\\n  concurrently. Gate: review N must complete **before slice N+1 merges** — a\\n  defect found in N's review must still be fixable without N+1 having built\\n  on it. Judgment is required on what N+1 actually built on; tightly-coupled\\n  slices serialize.\\n- **Shared ledgers scale with concurrency**: the pre-push row-count check on\\n  append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`; orchestrator.md Workflow\\n  step 10) is more important under parallel review, not less — parallel\\n  review means parallel merges (the 2026-10-25 corruption precedent:\\n  merge 4d6fd818 silently dropped ~66 ledger rows by replacing the file).\\n- Never relax the independence rule or skip reviews on small slices; the fix\\n  for throughput is more reviewers, not fewer reviews.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log:9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files -g '*1794600000000*' -g '*attribution*' -g '*contracts*' -g '*ADDENDUM*'; rg -n '8\\\\\\\\.1|address-linked adaptation|durable correspondence|caller-site' docs orchestrator-logs | head -100\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-DRAFT.md\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\ncollab/yang-python/1793410000000-orchestrator-yang-pyt