ts typeof :135, ts member-call :1167, and php ~:4229 (verify-first; possibly no code needed there). Smoke-test duty on shiva post-sweep per the reciprocal note. Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n2026-11-05 0x:xx +0700 — owner routing update: codex, agy, muse, qwen available; kimi CLI out (kimi via qwen allowed)\\nOwner: \\\"codex, agy, muse, qwen cli and their models are available. kimi cli is out, but you may use kimi from qwen cli.\\\" Measured: qwen probe OK (plan mode); implementation form is --approval-mode auto-edit, review form plan (reference lines 429-433). Board: implementation = muse-spark-1.3-contributor (proven) and qwen models (new capacity); architect = gpt-6.1-sol unchanged; agy = quota-walled until ~Nov 9-10; codex = available (sol primarily the architect per the earlier ruling; implementation-capable when the architect lane is idle). Also read: build-n-test.md boundary reconciliation (individual tests local; suite-scale remote only). Entered by the interactive ZCode seat (GLM-5.3-Flash).\\n---\\ndescription: Lead System Architect role definition for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# ROLE: Lead System Architect\\n\\n## Domain Scope\\n\\n- Foundational axioms, architectural invariants, and design contracts\\n- Subsystem boundaries, interpreters, host isolation, and explicit causality\\n- Cross-platform architecture, migration strategy, and compatibility boundaries\\n- ADR coherence, domain vocabulary, and theoretical grounding where useful\\n- Architecture review for high-risk or cross-cutting changes\\n- Commit sign-off: the sign-off on completed, review-reconciled work that\\n  authorizes the orchestrator to stage, commit, merge, and push it\\n  (trivial direct edits are exempt; see\\n  [`orchestrator.md`](orchestrator.md#coordination-contract))\\n\\nThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts it may\\ninspect or change and any permitted expansion.\\n\\n## Core Responsibilities\\n\\n1. **Protect Invariants**: Strictly enforce the 6 non-negotiable invariants (no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no raw callbacks, no shared mutable state, no layer collapsing, no assumed graphs).\\n2. **Decompose Complexity**: When new requirements emerge, decompose them into append-only streams, explicit interpreters, and immutable datom representations.\\n3. **Malleability Governance**: Ensure architecture adheres to bounded complexity, loose coupling, and high cohesion as defined in [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../malleability.md).\\n4. **Lineage Alignment**: Keep designs grounded in Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp architectural lineage.\\n\\n## Delegation Prompt Template\\n\\n```text\\nCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\n\\n# Task: <Task Name>\\n\\nRole: Lead System Architect\\n\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <local timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\\n\\nPerform a read-only architecture review of <design-change>.\\n\\nRead first:\\n- docs/design/datom.world.md\\n- <target-design-file>\\n- <relevant-implementation-boundary>\\n- <relevant-contract-tests>\\n\\nEvaluate foundational invariants, ownership boundaries, explicit state and\\ncontrol flow, concurrency and linearization, dynamic extension, host isolation,\\nCLJ/CLJS/CLJD portability, migration risk, completion criteria, and design\\ncontradictions. Distinguish architectural defects from implementation gaps or\\nintentionally deferred work. Do not edit files.\\n\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\n\\nThen report: severity | file:line | invariant/evidence | recommended correction.\\nAlso confirm the requested properties that passed review.\\n```\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1/collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log:16:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_7\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-11-05 03:40:00 GMT (v1)\\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-05 18:20:00 GMT (v2 — astra's four findings)\\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-05 20:10:00 GMT (v3 — qwen3.8-max's five amendments)\\nRevised-GMT: 2026-11-06 02:45:00 GMT (v4 — kimi k3.1-pro's four amendments; verdict: endorse for owner sign-off with these)\\n# RULING OF RECORD — attribution-as-query: caller-side site tagging; record, then attribute by query\\n\\nStatus: SIGNED by the owner (2026-11-06, \\\"i sign-off\\\") after three independent reviews (gpt-6-astra v1: 4 findings; qwen3.8-max v2: 5 amendments; kimi k3.1-pro v3: 4 amendments — all incorporated). This document is now the ruling-of-record and the implementation contract.\\n\\nReview state: v1 → astra (4 findings); v2 → qwen3.8-max (5 amendments); v3 → kimi k3.1-pro (4 amendments, below). Architecture settled across all three; v4's changes are mechanical corrections to the mechanism and the battery, per kimi: \\\"(1) caller-side AST tagging; (2) case 5 redefined + a seventh case; (3) the trace-shape-induced revision of ALL pins and the accounting rewrite; (4) R1's trigger and format conditions.\\\" Central principle (all three accept): derive credit from recorded relationships, require actual body entry in both lanes, refuse ambiguity. Unitarity preserves what flows through the pipeline; it cannot recover provenance never recorded — this ruling records the missing provenance minimally, then attributes by query.\\n\\n## R1 — The durable correspondence record is DESCOPED, on stated conditions\\n\\nAs v3: the address-linked adaptation manifest is deferred — the adapter is inlined per fixture, addresses need canonicalization nothing yet consumes, and no second consumer exists. TWO CONDITIONS from kimi's review: (a) **the deferred trigger is an OWNER/ARCHITECT call** — \\\"a second consumer exists\\\" is never self-declared by an implementer; (b) **the declared site tables are recorded in a stable, versioned evidence format** so the later R1 landing is an addition to the record, not a migration of it. The long-term stream-citizen direction stands as design intent. The offset table `kernel-context-extra-arguments` is retired — BUT its retirement has mechanical scope (see R6): it serves the context-API arities across the whole family, and its consumers (kernel-executed-arity? and ~30 pinned assertions) are rewritten with it.\\n\\n## R2 — The mechanism: CALLER-SIDE site tagging in the compiled AST; the callee record carries the caller's site id\\n\\n**Site ids are assigned at APPLICATION EXPRESSIONS in the compiled AST, post-macroexpansion** — not at callee body entry, and not on raw source forms (the corpus runs under `is`/`testing`/LaTTe macros; source-level tagging would silently miss every macro-expanded call site). The callee-entry trace record is extended to carry the **caller's site id**: the record becomes `[fname arity caller-site-id]`. Rationale (kimi's finding): the ambiguity is one guest callee clause serving two upstream arities — callee-side extension yields identical records for both origins and cannot work. Consequence for the scoping inventory: the **walker/semantic ingress row is updated** — the walker gains site-id stamping at application nodes; it is not \\\"nothing further.\\\" Indirect applications (`(:implicit-fn implicit-def)` and kin) have static expression positions, so they receive static site ids like any other call; the callee being runtime-unknown does not affect the id. Recursion is safe (existence-based consumers). The `/3` self-delegation gets its own unmapped site id → no credit — the originating-call rule (R3) is expressible only under this caller-side form, which is why the ruling mandates it.\\n\\n## R3 — Disjoint attribution patterns, verified by caller-site linkage\\n\\nA body entry attributes to upstream `/1` only via a site whose compiled argument expression is the ambient literal (`empty-env` supplied by the adapter's declared path); explicit-context sites attribute to `/2`. Disjointness is by recorded site identity and compiled argument shape, never by value equality.\\n\\n## R2b — The refusal battery: SEVEN cases\\n\\n1. An explicit `/2` call supplying an empty environment must NOT credit `/1`.\\n2. An unexecuted ambient `/1` beside an executed explicit `/2`: no borrowing.\\n3. Mismatched program/adaptation evidence: attribution refuses. **The referent is defined**: the mismatch is the canonical-source-hash of the inlined adapter, computed by the compiler during instrumentation and recorded per fixture — a trace from a fixture whose recorded hash differs refuses.\\n4. Missing-lane evidence: attribution refuses.\\n5. **REDEFINED** (kimi: the v3 case was unimplementable — the tagged trace records no arguments): the fixture's declared call shape is verified against the **compiled AST argument expression at that site** — a site declared ambient whose AST argument is not the ambient literal refuses. The check is compile-time, not value-sniffing.\\n6. Indirect-origin: body entries originating through indirect application refuse unless site-linked; default refusal (static ids make indirect sites linkable — they are not exempt).\\n7. **NEW (kimi)**: variable/derived argument shapes — a site whose context argument is a variable (bound to `empty-env` on one path, a populated env on another) is statically unclassifiable: **default refusal or `/2`-only attribution**; the declaration is checked against the AST argument expression (an `empty-env` literal may claim ambient; anything else may not).\\n8. **NEW (kimi, diagnostic)**: a declared site whose id never appears in any trace (stale table after a fixture edit) is a RECORDED DIAGNOSTIC — a mistyped table must not masquerade as honest refusal.\\n\\nThe `/3` self-delegation: the delegating call gets its own unmapped site id → no credit; the originating call is the credited event.\\n\\n## R4 — The attribution contract is versioned where the rule lives\\n\\nUnchanged from v3: the differential gate record (slice9_gate.md) versions the attribution section; `yin.cic.spec` v1 is untouched; \\\"mapped\\\" is defined by the declared site tables + caller-site-tagged records; name+arity matching is the degenerate 1:1 optimization.\\n\\n## R5 — Closure is MEASURED; the revision scope is HONEST about its blast radius\\n\\nThe attribution may expose unsupported credit among previously credited arities; valid witnesses preserved, withdrawn witnesses reported, H3-6 blocked until independent reconstruction succeeds. **The revision scope (kimi's correction of v3's under-statement): the trace shape change (2-vector → 3-vector) touches EVERY pin and path set built as 2-vector literals** — the refusal pins (`contains?` assertions on `[registered-definition? 2]`), every `kernel-executed-arity?` path set across the 20-arity family's ~30 assertions, and the gate-status computation (which must learn the third field or the credit set misparses). All of it is authorized by this ruling and listed in the evidence. \\\"No valid pin regresses\\\" means: pins encoding current semantics do not *silently* regress; pins requiring mechanical shape updates are updated and listed; pins encoding superseded semantics are revised on the record.\\n\\n## R6 — The retirement of the offset table is SCOPED to its real consumers\\n\\n`kernel-context-extra-arguments` serves the context-API arities across the family (not just F2) and is consumed by `kernel-executed-arity?`. Retiring it means **rewriting the accounting predicate** — the declared site tables must cover every context-API arity it served, or the retirement is partial and two mechanisms coexist (the failure qwen's amendment 4 warned against). The implementation round states the coverage list explicitly before deleting the table. The refusal pins' shape updates are covered by R5's authorization. (v3's \\\"the refusal pins are untouched\\\" is corrected: they are *mechanically updated* to the 3-vector shape, on the record.)\\n\\n## R7 — H3-6's audit runs the query — and re-derives the site tables\\n\\nUnchanged from v3, plus kimi's trust-model hardening: **the declared site tables are DERIVED MECHANICALLY from the compiled AST during instrumentation** (the compiler sees each site's argument expression), and H3-6's independent audit **re-derives them** from the same record — a wrong declaration is a compile/audit failure, not an honor-system risk.\\n\\n## Scoping inventory (astra + qwen + kimi reviewed)\\n\\n| Layer | Present now | Missing for the query |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Source/adaptation | pinned upstream source, `context.edn`, host assembly + rewrites | declared site tables (mechanically derived; versioned format); retired offset table |\\n| Compilation | `kernel-compile` AST per fixture (adapter INLINED; canonical source hash computed) | CALLER-SIDE site-id stamping at application nodes (post-expansion); per-fixture hash recording |\\n| Walker/semantic ingress | AST → datoms appended to a program medium | **UPDATED**: application-node site-id stamping in the walker; callee records extended with the caller's site id |\\n| Execution evidence | ordered body-entry pairs, both C lanes, outcomes | the third trace field (caller site id); the declared site tables |\\n| Trace/budget | `stream/make` 1000000; 2M-bound precedent | restated capacity + instruction bound for the added field |\\n| Gate accounting | manifest-derived denominator, name/arity matching; 2-vector trace literals throughout | `dao.space.query/q` attribution + the site tables + the 3-vector migration |\\n| Manifest schema | schema-1, CLOSED key set | nothing (linkage evidence is out-of-band; schema-2 only if R1's durable record later lands) |\\n\\n## Acceptance\\n\\n- The SEVEN-case battery + the stale-table diagnostic: all refusals refuse; the disjoint patterns attribute correctly on both lanes.\\n- The attribution reconstructs the credit set; withdrawn witnesses reported; 165/165 only if every attribution is supported.\\n- The pin revisions (mechanical shape updates + the semantic /1 re-credit) listed in the evidence; the `:gate-status` assertion PASSES for the first time; H3-6's independent audit re-derives the site tables and completes closure with final architect sign-off.\\n- No valid pin silently regresses; prior rulings stand; the trace capacity restated before any tagged-trace run; R1's deferred trigger reserved to the owner/architect.\\n\\n## Implementation ordering\\n\\nFirst deliverable: the written inventory confirmation (the instrumentation touch points, the capacity measurement, the offset-table coverage list). Then: caller-side site-id stamping in the walker/compile → the mechanically derived site tables → the seven-case battery + diagnostic → the accounting rewrite + pin migration → the attribution + re-credit → H3-6.\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"thread.started\\\",\\\"thread_id\\\":\\\"01a1acc9-5532-77a3-8d1a-3a11c6cca671\\\"}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"turn.started\\\"}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_0\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"I’ll check the draft against the signed ruling and architecture contracts, then get gpt-6-astra’s review of the five questions.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\n\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\n\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\n\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\n\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\n\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\n\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\n\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\n\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\n\\\\n## Streams\\\\n\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\n\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\n\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\n\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\n\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\n\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\n\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\n\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\n\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\n\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\n\\\\n## Code Style\\\\n\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\n\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\n\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\n\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\n\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\n\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md docs/agents/team.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md docs/agents/team.md'\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\ndescription: Lead Engineering Orchestrator role definition for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# ROLE: Lead Engineering Orchestrator\\\\n\\\\n## Domain Scope\\\\n\\\\n- Task scope, authorization boundaries, and phase completion criteria\\\\n- Complete ownership of task routing and role assignments as defined in [`team.md`](../team.md)\\\\n- Timestamped file-based handoffs, the append-only work log, session reuse,\\\\n  and delegated-agent patience\\\\n- Independent verification, finding reconciliation, and consensus\\\\n- Verification and commit-readiness reporting\\\\n\\\\nThis role owns no permanent file list. Each task defines the artifacts under\\\\ncoordination and the authority granted to every participant.\\\\n\\\\n## Scope of judgment\\\\n\\\\nThis role is a secretary, not a decision-maker: it tracks status, routes\\\\nwork, runs mechanical verification, and makes simple, reversible decisions —\\\\nnever complex judgment calls, which are always escalated to an Architect\\\\ndelegate (design/architectural questions) or the user (authorization, scope,\\\\npriority questions). This scoping is what makes the role viable on a small\\\\nor fast model: a flash-tier model can reliably do the secretarial half of\\\\nthis job, but cannot reliably weigh architectural tradeoffs or judge how far\\\\nan ambiguous instruction reaches.\\\\n\\\\nConcretely:\\\\n\\\\n- **Simple, orchestrator-owned decisions:** which delegate to route a task to\\\\n  (per `team.md`'s routing rules), whether local verification passed, whether\\\\n  a reviewer's finding is already reconciled, file/artifact bookkeeping,\\\\n  whether to retry a quiet-but-healthy delegate, and implementing small,\\\\n  low-risk changes directly per Workflow step 4 (e.g. a typo fix, a\\\\n  mechanical rename, a doc cross-reference update) rather than delegating\\\\n  them — \\\\\\\"simple\\\\\\\" here means no choice between competing approaches and no\\\\n  effect on behavior beyond the literal request.\\\\n- **Complex, always escalated decisions:** any architectural or design\\\\n  tradeoff, or any implementation choice that involves picking between\\\\n  competing approaches rather than a single obvious mechanical change (route\\\\n  to an Architect delegate, don't reason it out yourself); whether an\\\\n  ambiguous instruction authorizes a specific consequential action it didn't\\\\n  state explicitly — e.g. does \\\\\\\"make the edit\\\\\\\" also authorize committing it,\\\\n  does \\\\\\\"review this\\\\\\\" also authorize implementing the fix (ask the user,\\\\n  don't infer); weighing a reviewer's finding against the design's own\\\\n  intent when they conflict (route back to the Architect or the user, don't\\\\n  adjudicate it); any call that would change scope, authorization\\\\n  boundaries, or what \\\\\\\"done\\\\\\\" means for the current task (ask the user).\\\\n- When genuinely unsure which bucket a decision falls in, treat it as\\\\n  complex and escalate — the cost of an unnecessary question is far lower\\\\n  than the cost of an orchestrator-level model making an architectural or\\\\n  authorization call it wasn't equipped to make.\\\\n\\\\n## Coordination contract\\\\n\\\\nThe orchestrator owns scope, authorization, verification, consensus, and\\\\nreadiness; the Workflow section owns the sequence.\\\\n\\\\n**Commit authority** (2026-10-26 owner ruling). Staging, committing, merging\\\\nto master, and pushing are authorized by exactly one of:\\\\n\\\\n- **An Architect sign-off** ([`architect.md`](architect.md)) on completed\\\\n  work whose step 7 review has finished and whose findings are reconciled.\\\\n  The sign-off covers exactly the signed-off work, never unrelated changes\\\\n  that happen to be in the tree.\\\\n- **An explicit user instruction.** Only the user can waive step 7 review\\\\n  (2026-09-23 owner ruling).\\\\n- **The trivial-edit exemption.** A trivial direct edit as defined in Scope\\\\n  of judgment (a typo fix, a mechanical rename, a doc cross-reference\\\\n  update: no choice between approaches, no behavior change) needs neither\\\\n  review nor sign-off.\\\\n\\\\nLocal verification (step 6) is never a substitute for any of these. History\\\\nrewrites (amend, revert, reset) are outside all three, with one exception: an\\\\nunauthorized pre-review commit that is local, unpushed, and unmerged is\\\\nsoft-reset and the work goes through review, never reviewed after the fact.\\\\nAny other rewrite needs the user. That includes pushed work: a commit that\\\\nhas reached origin and then fails verification is reported to the user\\\\nimmediately with the evidence, and only the user authorizes its revert,\\\\nforward-fix, or force-push — the orchestrator never rewrites pushed history\\\\non its own authority.\\\\n\\\\nFor cost constraints and CLI routing caveats, see [`team.md`](../team.md)'s\\\\n**Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints** table. For current, session-\\\\nindependent owner-relayed availability and budget status (an outage, a\\\\nnarrowed authorization, a near-empty quota), see\\\\n[`routing-status.md`](../routing-status.md) — check it before routing to a\\\\nmetered destination, and add an entry when the owner relays a new status.\\\\n\\\\n**Delegate dispatches run locally by default (2026-11-03 owner ruling).**\\\\nA delegate CLI is invoked on THIS machine — the laptop — unless the work\\\\nitself must run elsewhere: test/nightly lanes on the remote hosts, or a\\\\nCLI that is genuinely missing locally (checked with `which <cli>` /\\\\n`<cli> models` first, never assumed). Never route a dispatch to a remote\\\\nhost because a previous round ran there, because the per-host model\\\\ncatalog was measured there, or for convenience: per-host catalogs are a\\\\nreason to run `<cli> models` wherever you dispatch, including locally,\\\\nbefore writing a model name into a brief. Remote dispatch adds scp and\\\\nssh round-trips to every turn and resume of the session, with no benefit\\\\nwhen the local checkout is current (push master first and it always is).\\\\nRemote dispatch adds scp and\\\\nssh round-trips to every turn and resume of the session, with no benefit\\\\nwhen the local checkout is current (push master first and it always is).\\\\n\\\\n**Long-running dispatches and remote gates wait via tracked background\\\\ntasks — never foreground sleeps.** Launch the delegate or the remote\\\\ngate so the host's task tracking owns the wait: in ZCode, run the\\\\ncommand with the harness's background mode (`run_in_background`), not\\\\ndetached via `nohup … &` inside a foreground call — a detached process\\\\nis invisible to the harness (no UI entry, no completion notification),\\\\nwhich is how dispatches went missing from the user's view (2026-11-04).\\\\nRemote gates launched through `bin/collab-tools/rverify.sh` run detached\\\\nin tmux on the host by design; wait for them with a background task that\\\\npolls the rverify `poll` subcommand on a long interval (≥ 5 min) and\\\\nexits when the DONE marker lands — one completion notification, no\\\\nforeground sleep chains. A foreground `sleep N; poll` chain blocks the\\\\nturn, risks the harness's command timeout killing the wait mid-poll,\\\\nand burns turns. If a foreground wait is cancelled or times out, the\\\\nremote run is NOT affected (tmux survives) — re-check on demand, never\\\\nrestart it. Report dispatch and completion as explicit status entries so\\\\nthe user sees every long-running task in the UI.\\\\n\\\\n## Artifact protocol\\\\n\\\\nCoordination is through repository artifacts, not hidden context. Artifacts\\\\nare named `<timestamp>-<role>-<task>.prompt.md`,\\\\n`<timestamp>-<role>-<task>.<sanitized-model>.findings.md`, and `<timestamp>-<role>-<task>.<sanitized-model>.stdout.log`.\\\\nThe `<timestamp>` prefix is a UNIX millisecond timestamp (e.g. `1725791234567`) so files sort\\\\nchronologically in Git's untracked view; `<sanitized-model>` prevents parallel reviewers from colliding.\\\\n\\\\n**Decide the topic before writing.** An artifact\\\\nis written directly into its destination topic subfolder\\\\n(`collab/yang-python/`, `collab/dev/`, … per step 11's folder rules) when\\\\nthe subsystem it belongs to is known — which is essentially always, since\\\\ndispatches are issued per track. The flat `collab/` root is reserved for\\\\nartifacts whose track is genuinely undetermined (cross-track orchestration\\\\nrulings, coordination whose destination is being decided), and filing them\\\\nis an explicit orchestrator decision, not a cleanup afterthought. The\\\\nmotivating incident: the 2026-11-03 worktree-deletion audit\\\\n(`orchestrator-logs/1793358019`) found that the only work lost when the\\\\n`-orch` worktree was deleted was unfiled flat-root collab logs, while a\\\\nfindings file written into `collab/jolt/` from day one survived. Unfiled\\\\nflat-root files are the fragile state; filed topic files are the durable\\\\none. In-flight vs archived is carried by GIT, not by the directory:\\\\nuntracked files (wherever they sit) are in-flight, committed files are the\\\\nrecord.\\\\n\\\\nEvery prompt starts with:\\\\n\\\\n```text\\\\nCreated-GMT: <actual timestamp>\\\\nCreated-Local: <actual timestamp and named timezone>\\\\nCoding-Agent: <claude|claude-cloud (claude-api lane)|codex|agy|glm|cmd|muse|deepseek|kimi|interactive>\\\\nSession-ID: <exact caller UUID | pending (provider-generated) | not-applicable (interactive seat)>\\\\n# Task: <name>\\\\nRole: <constant role>\\\\nImplementers:\\\\n- Model: <model> | Assigned: <timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nEvery report starts with the same header fields. Use actual timestamps; never fabricate them.\\\\n\\\\n`collab/` is append-only: artifacts are never edited after their round\\\\ncompletes, and `collab/` must never be added to `.gitignore` or\\\\n`.git/info/exclude` so its files stay visible and chronologically sorted in the user's Magit untracked\\\\nview. Artifacts stay untracked while in flight — whether they sit in the\\\\nflat root (undetermined track) or in their topic subfolder (the normal\\\\ncase, since the filing decision happens at write time) — and become the\\\\nrecord only through the archive step (Workflow step 11), which commits\\\\nthem. The two states live in\\\\ndifferent worlds: the committed topic subfolders are on the master\\\\nbranch (pushed to origin — master's collab/ subfolders are the durable,\\\\ndurable-per-seat-readable record), while an in-flight artifact exists\\\\nonly as an untracked file in the checkout that created it — invisible\\\\nfrom other checkouts and from every branch until it is committed. This\\\\nvisibility split is why a merged worktree's collab files must be\\\\ncommitted before that worktree is deleted (the worktree-deletion rule in\\\\nthe Worktree section below), and why in-flight artifacts belong in their\\\\ntopic subfolder from\\\\nbirth: a committed topic file survives checkout deletion, a flat-root\\\\nfile does not. Promote final responses to\\\\n`.findings.md`; `.stdout.log` is only\\\\nan intermediate capture.\\\\n\\\\nOn reassignment, append history inside `Implementers:`; never rewrite an earlier `Status:` line:\\\\n\\\\n```text\\\\n- Status-Event: <timestamp> | Model: <prior-model> | Status: <failed|timed-out|reassigned|superseded> | Rationale: <why>\\\\n- Model: <new-model> | Assigned: <timestamp> | Status: active | Rationale: <why>\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nUse `superseded` when a dispatch is killed or abandoned mid-flight because a new\\\\ninstruction changed what should be done, not because the delegate failed — this is a\\\\ndistinct case from `failed`/`timed-out` and should read that way in the artifact trail,\\\\nnot be inferred from a missing final report.\\\\n\\\\nRead-only reviewers may share the main tree. Concurrent editors use separate\\\\nworktrees from a committed base; uncommitted bases require serialization or\\\\nexplicit disjoint ownership (lane-sharing limits like \\\\\\\"only one process owns\\\\nthe CLJD lane\\\\\\\" are test-execution constraints owned by\\\\n[`build-n-test.md`](../build-n-test.md), not artifact rules). Auxiliary\\\\nworktrees lack\\\\n`collab/`, so briefs use absolute paths, and delegates there often stage\\\\ntheir own reference copies of prompts/findings in a local `collab/`. The\\\\ncanonical procedure makes copy-out unnecessary: collab files generated in a\\\\nworktree are committed there (step 11's `docs(collab):` commit) and reach\\\\nmaster through the merge. Before removing a worktree, diff its `collab/`\\\\nagainst the main tree's only as a safety check that no untracked file is\\\\nbeing lost — a worktree merge moves only committed content. A worktree\\\\nis deleted only after its work has been successfully merged to master\\\\nunder that authority — or on user instruction, which is a deliberate\\\\ndiscard. Because deletion follows the merge, the merged content is\\\\nalready canonical; confirm only that no other uncommitted file in the\\\\nworktree is being lost (a closed round has none: its artifacts were\\\\ncommitted at round-close). Never run `git checkout --` or `git clean`\\\\non a worktree just to make it removable: that destroys in-flight work\\\\n(observed 2026-10-26, when P3 oracle fixtures were lost this way).\\\\n\\\\nBatch complete briefs, reuse sessions for related follow-ups, start new sessions for unrelated work, and never rely on CLI `--last` flags. Session-ID generation, capture, and resume rules for\\\\nevery delegate CLI live in\\\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md#session-continuity),\\\\nnot here.\\\\n\\\\n## Work log\\\\n\\\\nEvery orchestrator session starts its own log at session start. Create it at\\\\nthe repository root as\\\\n`orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<coding-agent>-<model>-<brief-description>.md`,\\\\nusing the actual Unix timestamp (`date +%s`) and a short lowercase,\\\\nhyphen-separated description. Each orchestration is independent: never append\\\\nto `docs/orchestrator-log.md` or to another session's file, even when the new\\\\nsession continues an earlier task. `docs/orchestrator-log.md` is the frozen\\\\nlegacy record from before per-session logs existed — readable as historical\\\\nclaims only, never extended. When continuing a task, reference the prior\\\\nsession's log path in the new log's first entry and final handoff instead of\\\\nsharing or copying the file.\\\\n\\\\nThe current session's log is the seat's durable memory. Three entry kinds\\\\nonly: one per coherent unit (a delegated round, a fix, a review reconciliation,\\\\na readiness report), an unfinished-work entry when work stops before the next\\\\nunit finishes, and a final handoff entry when the seat is passed on. It records\\\\ncompleted and stopped units only; in-flight state lives in `collab/`,\\\\nreferenced by filename and session ID rather than duplicated. The\\\\nunfinished-work entry uses the same header schema, with the fields filled\\\\nfor what IS true: `Completed-GMT` timestamps the stop, `Done` names exactly\\\\nwhat landed and what did not, and the body ends with the remaining steps so\\\\na successor can continue from the artifact trail alone.\\\\n\\\\nStart every entry with actual timestamps and identity fields (never fabricate\\\\nthem), then state what `git log` alone cannot re-derive:\\\\n\\\\n```text\\\\n## <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name> — <task name>\\\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\\\nCoding-Agent: <one of the values in the prompt header above>\\\\nSession-ID: <exact Session-ID | not-applicable (interactive seat)>\\\\nTree: <branch>@<short-sha>, <committed | uncommitted changes: <files>>\\\\nDone: <what changed and why, with paths>\\\\nDecisions: <choices made, alternatives rejected, and their reasons>\\\\nVerification: <exact commands with outcomes and assertion counts; name unrun checks>\\\\nDelegates: <role/model, prompt and findings filenames, session IDs> | none\\\\nNext: <the next coherent unit, open risks, blockers>\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nWithin its owning session, the log is append-only: never edit, reorder, or\\\\ndelete an earlier entry — a correction is a new entry naming what it corrects.\\\\nWhen an entry names a `collab/` artifact, repeat its session ID so a successor\\\\ncan resume that session under the continuity rules above. The log may describe\\\\nwork that was never committed or has since been superseded: the tree is\\\\ncanonical, so a successor creates its own log and still re-derives state from\\\\n`git log`, `git status`, and the real diff, treating every prior log entry as a\\\\nclaim to verify rather than authority.\\\\n\\\\n## Authorization and security\\\\n\\\\nPrivate repository content may be sent externally only with explicit user\\\\nauthorization for the exact payload and destination. Invoke from the authorized\\\\nagent; consent does not carry over. Pass prompt paths or stdin, never private\\\\ndiffs or credentials in arguments. Use read-only/plan review and minimum write\\\\nscope; never bypass permission checks. Preserve unrelated changes and never\\\\nexpose or commit tokens or configuration. The same rule binds delegates: a\\\\ndelegate that requests a credential gets the requirement escalated to the\\\\nuser — never satisfied by pasting a secret into a prompt file or environment\\\\nthe orchestrator controls. Before an archive commit, scan the batch for\\\\ncredential-shaped strings (keys, tokens, `sk-`, bearer values); a stdout log\\\\nthat captured a secret is redacted or left uncommitted and the incident\\\\nrecorded in the work log.\\\\n\\\\n## Workflow\\\\n\\\\nThis section owns the execution sequence. Roster, role routing, and reviewer\\\\nindependence are defined in [`team.md`](../team.md); CLI flags and recipes are\\\\ndefined in [`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md);\\\\ncommit message format is defined in [`format.md`](../format.md#git-commit-messages).\\\\n\\\\n1. **Establish the seat.** The harness must be able to read and write the working\\\\n   tree, run every affected host's checks, and invoke delegate CLIs. Establish\\\\n   those capabilities before accepting work. At session start, create this\\\\n   session's own `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<coding-agent>-<model>-<brief-description>.md`\\\\n   file under the repository root as specified in Work log. If a required\\\\n   capability is missing, report it and do not issue a blind sign-off.\\\\n2. **Re-derive state and authority.** Read `git log`, `git status`, and the real\\\\n   diff; snapshots and phase summaries may be stale. Read\\\\n   [`docs/agents/routing-status.md`](../routing-status.md) for current\\\\n   owner-relayed delegate availability and budget constraints. When explicitly\\\\n   continuing an earlier orchestration, read its referenced prior log as\\\\n   historical claims, never as a file to append to. Bound the user's authorized\\\\n   files, tools, payloads, and external destinations.\\\\n3. **Define the contract.** Express the task as tests or equally precise\\\\n   acceptance criteria, invariants, phase-completion criteria, and bounded file\\\\n   ownership.\\\\n4. **Choose execution and review routes.** Follow the role selection and reviewer independence rules in\\\\n   [`team.md`](../team.md). Implement simple, low-risk work directly when delegation would cost more in\\\\n   coordination, tokens, or review. Otherwise, assign a specialized implementation role to the optimal\\\\n   model per `team.md`'s model tables; its cost-constraint table takes precedence over model fit. Add\\\\n   Architect or Security review when the role or risk requires it.\\\\n5. **Brief and execute.** When delegating, use the selected role's delegation\\\\n   prompt template — [`team.md`](../team.md)'s Roster table links each role\\\\n   name to its template file under `docs/agents/roles/`; do not improvise a\\\\n   brief from the roster's one-line description alone. Keep the brief\\\\n   concise and unambiguous. When it relays an owner\\\\n   instruction that is being reinterpreted, corrected, or applied to a design\\\\n   (not simply passed through verbatim), quote the owner's exact words and\\\\n   label them as a quote, kept visibly separate from your own paraphrase of\\\\n   what they mean — a paraphrase fused into one voice hides the gap where a\\\\n   misreading survives to execution instead of being caught first (this\\\\n   happened in this project's own history: an ambiguous \\\\\\\"no legacy support\\\\\\\"\\\\n   instruction was paraphrased as \\\\\\\"drop the old algorithm entirely,\\\\\\\" the\\\\n   opposite of what was meant, and nearly reached a delegate before the owner\\\\n   caught it). If a plausible alternate reading of the owner's words exists,\\\\n   ask before dispatching rather than letting a delegate execute an\\\\n   unconfirmed interpretation. A delegate's exit code or promise is not a\\\\n   deliverable: inspect the artifact and resume an unfinished turn. Quiet\\\\n   output is not failure; wait for completion or an explicit error. Do not\\\\n   terminate a healthy agent merely because it is quiet for several minutes\\\\n   (especially true for DeepSeek).\\\\n   - **Quiet vs. stalled is a procedure, not a judgment.** A delegate is\\\\n     stalled only when, for 15 minutes, its stdout log has not grown AND no\\\\n     live process matches it (`pgrep -f <cli>`); check the CLI's quirk entry\\\\n     in [`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md)\\\\n     before concluding (some quiet runs are healthy, some \\\\\\\"session in use\\\\\\\"\\\\n     messages mean the first run is still alive). A stalled run is killed,\\\\n     its artifact inspected for partial work, and the round recovered by\\\\n     resume-by-state or fresh session — never silently re-dispatched as if\\\\n     nothing happened.\\\\n   - **Delegate death has a terminal state.** After two failed rounds on the\\\\n     same unit (different sessions, and at least two different models where\\\\n     the roster allows), stop dispatching: record both failures in the work\\\\n     log with their artifact trails and escalate to the user with the\\\\n     evidence. A third model is chosen only on user instruction — an\\\\n     unbounded retry loop is the failure mode this rule exists to end.\\\\n6. **Verify — individual tests may run locally; suites run remotely (owner\\\\n   ruling 2026-11-04).** Inspect the actual artifact and diff in the\\\\n   orchestrator's environment. An individual test is fast: a single failing\\\\n   test or one namespace's reproduction (`-v <ns>/<var>`, `-n <ns>`,\\\\n   `--test=<ns>`, `--only <ns>`) may run on the laptop. Everything\\\\n   suite-scale — subsystem lane sets (G1), changed-set runs (G2), full\\\\n   suites, the slow tier, the nightly — runs on the remote hosts\\\\n   (shiva/jupiter): the suite has outgrown the laptop, and local suite runs\\\\n   are too slow and load the machine the owner is using. Delegate agents\\\\n   (`claude`, `codex`, `glm`, …) can execute on the remote test hosts\\\\n   directly — see [`build-n-test.md`](../build-n-test.md), section \\\\\\\"Remote\\\\n   agent execution\\\\\\\" (the laptop-unload rule: bookkeeping locally, execution\\\\n   remotely) and \\\\\\\"Remote test servers\\\\\\\" for the host recipes (scratch clones\\\\n   under `~/ci/`, mise activation order, per-host lane overlap limits).\\\\n   Delegated test claims are untrusted: the orchestrator reads the\\\\n   remote run's actual output (log paths on the host), not the delegate's\\\\n   summary. User-run results count as evidence only when the exact command,\\\\n   output, and tested revision are available; otherwise run the checks.\\\\n   Tell later reviewers which checks already passed so they spend their\\\\n   budget on static analysis instead of redundant suites, except when\\\\n   security review requires a rerun.\\\\n7. **Review and reconcile.** Give the verified diff to an independent reviewer\\\\n   before committing (exceptions: the coordination contract's user waiver and\\\\n   trivial-edit exemption). Weigh findings on their merits, fix accepted\\\\n   defects, and resume the same reviewer to confirm the correction. Preserve\\\\n   every round. Findings are reconciled when every one carries a recorded\\\\n   disposition: fixed and reviewer-confirmed, rejected with evidence, or\\\\n   deferred with user approval. A reviewer–architect dispute is terminated by\\\\n   one architect adjudication per unit: the ruling is final for that unit,\\\\n   and residual disagreement is recorded as an unresolved risk in the step 8\\\\n   readiness report — never re-litigated by re-dispatching review rounds.\\\\n8. **Report readiness.** Report exact commands, assertion counts, reviewer\\\\n   sign-off, unrun checks, unreviewed changes, unresolved risks, and any tool or\\\\n   hook noise. Do not let the summary outrun the evidence. Append the unit's\\\\n   entry to this session's own file under `orchestrator-logs/` in the same terms.\\\\n9. **Stage, commit, and merge only when authorized** under the coordination\\\\n   contract's commit authority. Stage only the authorized work, inspect the\\\\n   staged diff immediately before committing, and commit only that diff.\\\\n   Use the commit message format in\\\\n   [`format.md`](../format.md#git-commit-messages). Before staging in the\\\\n   shared main tree, re-run `git status` and compare against the state\\\\n   derived in step 2: any working-tree change not attributable to this\\\\n   seat's units (another seat's or the user's in-flight work) freezes the\\\\n   commit — escalate, never stage around it, and never `git add -A` in the\\\\n   main tree.\\\\n10. **Verify what landed.** Compare the commit's diff with the authorized\\\\n    staged diff. If hooks or formatters changed what landed, review that delta and\\\\n    rerun its affected checks — the pre-commit formatter hook can stage files\\\\n    you never added (observed 2026-10-26: an untracked in-flight file of\\\\n    another track was swept into a ledger commit; caught only by reading the\\\\n    post-commit `--stat` against intent). A failure means the work is not\\\\n    ready; rewrite history only as the coordination contract allows.\\\\n    **Shared append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`) have a merge\\\\n    rule** (2026-10-26, after merge 4d6fd818 silently dropped ~66 rows by\\\\n    replacing the file with a rewritten 16-row variant): never resolve a\\\\n    ledger conflict by taking one side's file, and never land a rewritten or\\\\n    reformatted ledger inside a feature merge. The ledger is append-only\\\\n    ROW-WISE: the only correct resolution is the union of both sides' rows\\\\n    under the existing column schema; a schema change is a proposal to the\\\\n    work log, agreed by seats, executed as a row-wise migration. Before\\\\n    pushing any commit or merge that touches the ledger, count rows on both\\\\n    parents and on the result — the result must be at least max(parents):\\\\n    `git show <rev>:docs/agents/known-gaps.md | grep -c '^|'`. A smaller\\\\n    number means you just dropped another seat's rows; restore per the union\\\\n    rule before pushing.\\\\n11. **Archive completed-task artifacts.** When collaboration artifacts are no\\\\n    longer needed for active collaboration (their task is committed or its\\\\n    track is otherwise closed), commit them — the storage model, the\\\\n    write-time filing rule, and the ruling history are canonical in the\\\\n    Artifact protocol; this step is only the mechanical procedure.\\\\n    Artifacts filed at write time are archived by `git add collab/<topic>/`\\\\n    plus the commit; the only files that need MOVING are flat-root\\\\n    stragglers whose track was undetermined at write time — move those into\\\\n    git-tracked, subsystem-organized subfolders of the same directory (e.g.\\\\n    `collab/yang-python/`, `collab/dao-stream/`, `collab/yin-ucf/`,\\\\n    `collab/yin-linker/`) under their exact filenames. Every archive batch\\\\n    is committed (and pushed) as `docs(archive):` or `docs(collab):`; an\\\\n    archive that is not committed is not an archive. Do not leave archived\\\\n    files untracked, and do not invent intermediate locations. Organize\\\\n    archived files by design subsystem, not by generic slice codes or\\\\n    temporary folder names:\\\\n\\\\n    - **The subsystem is defined by the code, not by the seat.** When the work\\\\n      belongs to a subsystem in [`src/dev/subsystems.edn`](../../../src/dev/subsystems.edn)\\\\n      (the same registry `bb test:sub` uses), the destination folder is that\\\\n      subsystem's name with dots replaced by dashes (`yang.python` ->\\\\n      `collab/yang-python/`, `yin.vm` -> `collab/yin-vm/`). Do not invent a\\\\n      variant spelling of a subsystem that exists.\\\\n    - **Check before creating.** Before creating any new destination folder,\\\\n      list `collab/`'s existing subfolders and reuse one when the work belongs\\\\n      there — including cross-cutting categories that predate the subsystem\\\\n      registry (`collab/orchestration/`, `collab/general/`, and track\\\\n      categories like `collab/dao-star/`, `collab/jolt/`). A new folder is\\\\n      created only when no existing subfolder fits; name it after the\\\\n      subsystem when there is one, otherwise after the durable track or topic\\\\n      (never a date, a slice code, or a seat name). When neither the registry\\\\n      nor an existing folder fits, propose the new folder name in the step 8\\\\n      readiness report for user or architect confirmation instead of creating\\\\n      it unilaterally — folder names are durable, and naming is a shared\\\\n      convention, not a per-seat choice.\\\\n    - **One artifact, one destination.** Same-named collisions resolve by\\\\n      content comparison, never by size or ending heuristics: identical bytes\\\\n      drop the duplicate; differing same-name artifacts are kept as suffixed\\\\n      variants (the `-b`, `-r2` convention) — neither copy dies.\\\\n\\\\n    Never delete, truncate, rename, or overwrite an artifact. Same-named\\\\n    collisions (in whatever folder they meet) resolve by the rule above;\\\\n    a `mv` that would land on an existing file leaves the collision in\\\\n    place for that resolution. Archive only committed work and only when\\\\n    no uncommitted tracked changes could make task ownership ambiguous.\\\\n\\\\nIf the remaining budget cannot finish the next coherent unit, leave the tree\\\\nreadable and record incomplete work in the findings rather than leaving a\\\\nhalf-applied edit, and append the unfinished-work entry naming what remains\\\\nso a successor can continue it. The trigger is explicit: when the seat\\\\nestimates its remaining budget is less than one more coherent unit requires,\\\\nit writes the unfinished-work entry and issues the Seat Handoff Template\\\\n(below) before attempting further work — it does not start a unit it cannot\\\\nfinish.\\\\n\\\\n**Blocked on the user: park, don't poll.** When progress requires a user\\\\nanswer (authorization, scope, an adjudication this doc reserves to the\\\\nuser), stop the work: park every delegate (no session left running or\\\\nmetered while idle — the 144-minute idle-session incident is the cost this\\\\nrule exists to end), record the blocker and the pending question in the\\\\nwork log's unfinished-work entry, and end the turn. The seat resumes only\\\\nwhen the user's answer arrives.\\\\n\\\\n## Orchestrator Seat Handoff Template\\\\n\\\\nUse this template only when handing the entire orchestrator seat to another\\\\nagent. Implementation and review delegates use their own role templates; they\\\\nmust not select or invoke further agents unless the user's authorization and\\\\ntheir brief explicitly grant that coordination role.\\\\n\\\\n```text\\\\nCreated-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\\\nCreated-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\\\nCoding-Agent: <coding-agent-or-cli>\\\\nSession-ID: <caller-generated UUID | pending (provider-generated) | not-applicable (interactive seat)>\\\\n\\\\n# Task: <Task Name>\\\\n\\\\nRole: Lead Engineering Orchestrator\\\\n\\\\nImplementers:\\\\n- Model: <model-name> | Assigned: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone> | Status: active | Rationale: Initial assignment\\\\n\\\\nCoordinate <phase-or-task> in <repository-root>.\\\\n\\\\nRead first:\\\\n- <governing-design-file>\\\\n- <current-phase-status>\\\\n- <relevant-source-and-test-files>\\\\n- <prior-orchestrator-log-path-if-explicit-continuation> (historical claims only; do not append)\\\\n- `docs/agents/routing-status.md` (current owner-relayed delegate availability and budget status)\\\\n- `docs/agents/delegate-invocation-reference.md` (session-ID mechanics, CLI flags, and recipes)\\\\n\\\\nEvery claim in this brief, in any prior orchestrator log, and in\\\\nrouting-status.md is something the outgoing seat believed at handoff time, not\\\\nverified fact — re-derive tree state yourself from `git log`, `git status`, and\\\\nthe real diff before acting on any of it, the same posture this role's own Work\\\\nLog section requires of any successor.\\\\n\\\\nRequired workflow:\\\\n- Follow `docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md#workflow` in order.\\\\n- Create a new session log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<coding-agent>-<model>-<brief-description>.md`; never append to the prior session's log.\\\\n- Run <focused-test-command> on the remote test hosts (shiva/jupiter) per Workflow step 6; <lint-command> may run locally.\\\\n\\\\nDo not broaden scope; do not stage, commit, or merge without an Architect\\\\nsign-off, an explicit user instruction, or the trivial-edit exemption\\\\n(see the Coordination contract above); do not trust\\\\ndelegated test claims without local evidence, or terminate a healthy agent\\\\nmerely because it is slow or temporarily quiet.\\\\n\\\\nBegin the final response exactly with:\\\\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\\\\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\\\\nCoding-Agent: <same coding agent used for the run>\\\\nSession-ID: <exact initial Session-ID value, with provider-generated value promoted after capture>\\\\n\\\\nThen report delegated roles/models, prompts and session IDs, verified findings,\\\\ntest outcomes, unresolved risks, and whether the phase is ready to commit.\\\\nAppend the final handoff entry to this session's new log before responding,\\\\nreferencing the prior session's log path when continuing earlier work.\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n## Delegate Invocation Reference\\\\n\\\\nCLI flags, canonical recipes, and known quirks for every delegate CLI\\\\n(claude, claude-cloud [the claude-api lane wrapper], glm, deepseek, muse, codex, cmd, agy, kimi) live in\\\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](../delegate-invocation-reference.md),\\\\nnot here — read it before shelling out to any delegate CLI. It is reference\\\\nmaterial needed at the moment of dispatch, not on every orchestrator turn,\\\\nwhich is why it is kept separate from this role's core identity and workflow.\\\\nDispatch is always by direct CLI invocation per that reference's canonical\\\\nrecipes. The legacy `dispatch.sh` helper has been deleted.\\\\n---\\\\ndescription: Team roster, role routing, and reviewer-independence rules for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD SOFTWARE ENGINEERING TEAM\\\\n\\\\nCanonical guide for the shared roster, role routing, and reviewer independence.\\\\nThe Lead Engineering Orchestrator owns coordination, authorization, artifact\\\\nprotocol, and verification in [`orchestrator.md`](./roles/orchestrator.md);\\\\ndelegate session protocol and CLI recipes live in\\\\n[`delegate-invocation-reference.md`](./delegate-invocation-reference.md).\\\\n\\\\n## Roster\\\\n\\\\nThe Orchestrator is deliberately absent from this table; it is the user-facing\\\\nagent that centrally routes tasks to these roles. Each role name links to its\\\\ndelegation prompt template under `docs/agents/roles/`. Review is one role\\\\nbacked by one file, `roles/reviewer.md`, covering adversarial, routine, and\\\\nsecurity-sign-off review via its Adversarial Review and Consensus Follow-up\\\\ntemplates — adjust scope and rigor in the brief per flavor, not the file.\\\\n\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Role                                              | Responsibility                                                                                         |\\\\n+===================================================+========================================================================================================+\\\\n| [Architect](roles/architect.md)                   | Axioms, invariants, boundaries, architecture                                                           |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [Compiler & AST](roles/compiler-engineer.md)      | AST, lowering, compile-time macros                                                                     |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [Frontend & Graphics](roles/graphics-engineer.md) | Events, WebGL/WebGPU, canvas, terminal                                                                 |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [QA & Verification](roles/qa-engineer.md)         | TDD, parity, lint, regression                                                                          |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [Review](roles/reviewer.md)                       | Adversarial defect discovery, routine correctness/portability checks, and security capability sign-off |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [Scoped / Subagent](roles/subagent.md)            | Bounded searches, edits, docs, lint                                                                    |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [Storage & Indexing](roles/storage-engineer.md)   | B-trees, indexing, DHT, storage, query                                                                 |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [Stream & Network](roles/stream-engineer.md)      | Streams, framing, concurrency, transports, codecs, RPC                                                 |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| [VM Runtime](roles/vm-engineer.md)                | CESK, VMs, continuations, macros, loops                                                                |\\\\n+---------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n## Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints\\\\n\\\\nThe Orchestrator routes work using these cost profiles. Flat subscriptions are preferred; metered models are reserved for tasks explicitly requiring them.\\\\n\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Provider        | Cost Structure                         | Routing Notes                                                                                               |\\\\n+=================+========================================+=============================================================================================================+\\\\n| agy             | Flat: Included                         | Treated as a flat subscription. Use freely within budget.                                                   |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| qwen            | Token Plan subscription (Alibaba Cloud | The `qwen` CLI is Alibaba's own coding agent (`@qwen-code/qwen-code@0.17.0`, npm-global under nvm node      |\\\\n|                 | Model Studio, ap-southeast-1)          | v26.1.0), not a Claude Code wrapper. Headless dispatch and session mechanics measured (2026-11-04): caller  |\\\\n|                 |                                        | `--session-id <uuid>`, follow-up `qwen --resume <uuid>` (round-trip verified; chats stored per project      |\\\\n|                 |                                        | under ~/.qwen/projects/). Implementation requires `--approval-mode auto-edit`: the default mode silently    |\\\\n|                 |                                        | no-ops tools headless. Config `~/.qwen/settings.json`: key in env `BAILIAN_TOKEN_PLAN_API_KEY`; gateway     |\\\\n|                 |                                        | https://token-plan.ap-southeast-1.maas.aliyuncs.com/compatible-mode/v1 (the key 403s at cn-beijing).        |\\\\n|                 |                                        | Measured catalog: `qwen3.8-max` (default), `qwen3.8-flash`, `qwen3.7-max`, `qwen3.6-flash`,                 |\\\\n|                 |                                        | `deepseek-v4-pro`; all others denied or absent. Routing status: available NOW (coding round-trip passed:    |\\\\n|                 |                                        | files written, assertions passed, resume verified).                                                         |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| claude          | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Subscription stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| claude-api      | Inactive / Cancelled                   | Anthropic API access stopped by owner. Do not route tasks here.                                             |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| codex           | Flat: ChatGPT Plus ($20/mo)            | Strict rate limits. Available for any role (architecture, implementation, review, QA).                      |\\\\n|                 |                                        | All codex models (astra, sol, terra, luna) draw ONE shared pool: a cut darkens every seat at once, so       |\\\\n|                 |                                        | sequence codex dispatches rather than fanning them out. Independent parallel capacity is codex | kimi | glm.|\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| deepseek        | Metered: Pay-by-token                  | Flash: $0.14/M in, $0.28/M out. Pro: $0.435/M in, $0.87/M out.                                              |\\\\n|                 |                                        | Off-peak (10:00-01:00, 04:00-06:00 UTC & wknds) is 50% cheaper.                                             |\\\\n|                 |                                        | Cache hits are ~50x cheaper. Reserve for necessary work.                                                    |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| glm             | Flat: Pro yearly plan ($672/yr)        | Peak hours: weekdays 14:00-18:00 UTC+8. Schedule large jobs off-peak (50% rate).                            |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| kimi            | Flat: Kimi membership (Plus+)          | Invoke only through the dedicated `kimi` CLI. Monthly quotas apply.                                         |\\\\n|                 |                                        | Pro unlocks Highspeed and 1M context for K3.x.                                                              |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| mistral         | Flat: Mistral Pro ($20/mo)             | Invoke via `vibe` CLI. API-live aliases (probed 2026-11-04): `mistral-4.0` (the current large-4 generation; |\\\\n|                 |                                        | use it for large-model work) and `mistral-medium-3.5`. The bare `mistral-large` alias is DEAD at the API:   |\\\\n|                 |                                        | the CLI parses it but the live API answers 400 invalid_model (same for `devstral` and `codestral`);         |\\\\n|                 |                                        | anything else (`mistral-small`, `magistral-*`, `mixtral-*`) is not even CLI-recognized.                     |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| muse            | Meta login / `META_API_KEY`            | Invoke via `muse` CLI; headless form is `muse exec`. Models:                                                |\\\\n|                 |                                        | `muse-wonder-1.0` (flagship) and `muse-spark-1.3` (fast lane).                                              |\\\\n|                 |                                        | Check [`routing-status.md`](./routing-status.md) before dispatch (geo-restricted at last relay).            |\\\\n+-----------------+----------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n\\\\n## Model Strengths and Selection Guide\\\\n\\\\nThe following guide details the strengths, weaknesses, and optimal use cases for each model based on their current (late 2026) technical capabilities.\\\\n\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| Model                       | Strengths & When to Use                                            | Weaknesses & When Not to Use                                        |\\\\n+=============================+====================================================================+=====================================================================+\\\\n| `claude-fable-5-1`          | Highest capability for long-horizon agentic work, complex research,| Slower inference; resource hog (rate limits); mandatory             |\\\\n|                             | strict boundaries, and managing vast context (efficient caching).  | watermarking. **Avoid for:** Routine fast-loop tasks; precise       |\\\\n|                             | **Best for:** Architecture definition, security sign-offs.         | one-shot implementations where strict rigor is overkill.            |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `claude-opus-5.5`           | Performs at Fable 5.1 levels for most work at markedly lower cost  | Still higher latency/cost than Sonnet 5. **Avoid for:** Fast QA     |\\\\n|                             | than the prior Opus generation. **Best for:** Code migration,      | loops or simple code generation where Sonnet 5 suffices.            |\\\\n|                             | software optimization, and complex agentic coding.                 |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `claude-sonnet-5-5`         | Frontier agentic workhorse; substantial gains in reasoning depth,  | Can struggle with high-order axiomatic proofs or subtle security    |\\\\n|                             | instruction following, and rigorous code audits. Low latency and   | capability proofs vs Fable 5.1 / Astra. **Avoid for:** Final        |\\\\n|                             | high cost-efficiency. **Best for:** Core implementation, deep QA   | architectural sign-offs on high-risk cryptographic or consensus     |\\\\n|                             | adversarial code reviews, multi-file refactors, and test suites.   | boundaries where Opus 5.5 / Fable 5.1 / Astra is mandated.          |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `deepseek-flash`            | V4.1-Flash outpaces V4-Pro in speed, cost, and agentic benchmarks. | Can hallucinate on deep invariant constraints. **Avoid for:**       |\\\\n|                             | **Best for:** QA parity checks, scoped subagent work, and          | Complex system design, AST lowering.                                |\\\\n|                             | high-efficiency native multimodal tasks.                           |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `deepseek-v4-pro`           | Legacy 1.6T MoE architecture. **Best for:** Advanced logic         | Actively being superseded by V4.1-Flash; high cost and undercooked  |\\\\n|                             | workflows and adversarial reviews.                                 | relative to size. **Avoid for:** General use (use Flash instead).   |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gemini-3.8-flash`          | Lightning-fast; highly cost-efficient; 1M+ context with native     | Can oversimplify subtle invariant edge cases vs frontier models.    |\\\\n|                             | multimodal support. **Best for:** High-volume agentic loops,       | **Avoid for:** Core security capability enforcement; top-level      |\\\\n|                             | visual/frontend inspection, rapid TDD/QA, and subagents.           | architecture.                                                       |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gemini-4-argon`            | Frontier reasoning with configurable effort and deep synthesis     | Higher latency/cost at medium/high effort than Flash-tier models;   |\\\\n|                             | across large multi-file specs and codebases; strong architectural  | sandboxed headless CLI cannot run local JVM (`clojure`) suites.     |\\\\n|                             | judgment, invariant verification, and surgical cross-checking.     | **Avoid for:** High-volume trivial edits, mechanical sweeps, or     |\\\\n|                             | **Best for:** System architecture, design/code review, complex     | headless tasks requiring in-session JVM test execution.             |\\\\n|                             | debugging, and multi-step implementation.                          |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `glm-5.3`                   | Scaled post-training for exceptional long-horizon tasks and        | Heavier footprint; can overcomplicate simple data framing. **Avoid  |\\\\n|                             | cybersecurity. **Best for:** Complex programming workflows, VMs,   | for:** UI/UX, canvas, or WebGPU tasks.                              |\\\\n|                             | storage, indexing, DHTs, and networking.                           |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `glm-5.3-flash`             | High performance at a lower computational cost; steep discounts    | Limited complex reasoning on novel architectures; self-assessment   |\\\\n|                             | off-peak and weekend boosts. Excels when claims are checkable      | is unreliable — judge it behaviorally. **Avoid for:** Core          |\\\\n|                             | against an authority: evidence gathering, citation verification,   | architecture, unbounded design authorship, peak-hour execution if   |\\\\n|                             | reconciliation, protocol discipline. **Best for:** Scoped          | budget is tight.                                                    |\\\\n|                             | subagents, bounded searches, QA spot-checks, verification seats.   |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gpt-5.4-mini`              | Fast, lower-cost model for bounded, repetitive work with explicit  | Do not use as the sole judge of subtle invariants or broad design.  |\\\\n|                             | acceptance criteria. **Best for:** TDD loops, simple linting,      | Escalate multi-step refactors and disputed findings to Terra, Sol,  |\\\\n|                             | extraction, and scoped text edits.                                 | or Astra.                                                           |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gpt-6-astra`               | Most capable end-to-end model. **Best for:** Hardest architecture, | Highest cost. Reserve for the highest-risk or most difficult work   |\\\\n|                             | security, research, coding, computer-use, and long-horizon tasks.  | under the subscription policy.                                      |\\\\n|                             | Capability includes implementation across any role.                |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gpt-6-luna`                | Most affordable GPT-6 model; optimized for high-volume tasks and   | Low-cost does not mean suitable for unbounded design or the hardest |\\\\n|                             | fast response times. Improved factuality and coding. **Best for:** | invariant reasoning. Escalate complex architecture/security.        |\\\\n|                             | High-volume tasks, extraction, scoped searches, and routine QA.    |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gpt-6.1-sol`               | Intelligent everyday workhorse with near-Astra intelligence, 1M+   | More expensive than Luna. Use Astra when the task is the hardest    |\\\\n|                             | context window, and 95% prompt cache discount. Upgraded reasoning  | end-to-end problem requiring frontier architectural verification.   |\\\\n|                             | effort control (low to max), +6.4% DeepSWE / +7% OSWorld gains over|                                                                     |\\\\n|                             | predecessor. **Best for:** Complex implementation, advanced coding,|                                                                     |\\\\n|                             | high-stakes security, and architectural review.                    |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `gpt-6.1-terra`             | Balanced intelligence and cost. **Best for:** Default production   | Use Sol or Astra for the hardest reasoning, high-risk security, or  |\\\\n|                             | engineering, integration work, network streams, VM runtimes,       | architecture decisions. Validate compiler-lowering and other        |\\\\n|                             | moderate-complexity implementation, and subagent fallback.         | specialist work with focused tests and independent review.          |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `kimi-for-coding-highspeed` | K2.7 Code Highspeed; 5-6x faster output with 256K context.         | Uses about 3x quota; fixed thinking; Pro tier required. **Avoid     |\\\\n|                             | **Best for:** Latency-sensitive routine coding and iteration.      | for:** 1M-context work or tasks needing adjustable reasoning.       |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `k3.1-pro`                  | Latest 3T-class flagship; 1M context, image/video input, and       | Higher resource use than K3 256K; thinking is mandatory; 1M         |\\\\n|                             | strongest long-horizon agentic coding. **Best for:** Hardest       | context requires Pro. **Avoid for:** Routine Clojure or latency-    |\\\\n|                             | implementation, large refactors, architecture, deep review, and    | sensitive loops; use K3 256K or Highspeed instead. Also avoid       |\\\\n|                             | difficult multi-file Clojure, compiler, VM, and invariant work.    | clients that cannot keep thinking enabled.                          |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `k3.1-pro-highspeed`        | Same K3.1 Pro capabilities with 2-3x faster output and 1M          | About 2x quota usage; Pro tier; accelerates model output, not       |\\\\n|                             | context. **Best for:** Responsive complex coding, long-horizon     | tools. **Avoid for:** Quota-sensitive or tool-bound work where      |\\\\n|                             | agents, and iterative difficult debugging.                         | regular K3.1 Pro has the same quality at lower quota cost.          |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `k3`                        | Previous 2.8T flagship; 1M context, image/video input, adjustable  | Superseded by K3.1 Pro; 1M uses about 2x the quota of K3 256K       |\\\\n|                             | thinking. **Best for:** Large-codebase analysis, multi-file        | and requires Pro. **Avoid for:** Hardest tasks when K3.1 Pro is     |\\\\n|                             | refactors, and long-document processing.                           | available, or small jobs that fit K3 256K.                          |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `k3-256k`                   | Same K3 results within 256K at about half the 1M-context quota.    | 256K limit; image input only, no video. **Avoid for:** Vast         |\\\\n|                             | Most quota-efficient Clojure choice; use low thinking for routine  | contexts, long documents, video, or difficult multi-file Clojure,   |\\\\n|                             | REPL fixes, features, and small/medium refactors. **Best for:**    | compiler, VM, and invariant work; use K3.1 Pro for those instead.   |\\\\n|                             | routine coding, code completion, Q&A, and small edits.             |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `mistral-4.0`               | The current large-4 generation and this lane's API-live large      | Context window not probe-verified from this seat (the reachable     |\\\\n|                             | alias (probed 2026-11-04). Strong general reasoning and code       | provider docs predate the 4.0 generation); probe before             |\\\\n|                             | generation. **Best for:** General implementation, refactors, and   | long-document work. **Avoid for:** Deep invariant proofs; use Fable |\\\\n|                             | code review on the mistral lane.                                   | 5.1/Opus 5.5 on the highest-stakes cryptographic boundaries.        |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `mistral-medium-3.5`        | API-live via `vibe` (probed 2026-11-04); the lane's efficient      | Lighter reasoning than the mistral-4.0 generation (tier             |\\\\n|                             | mid-tier. **Best for:** Routine coding, QA, extraction, and scoped | positioning; not benchmarked here). **Avoid for:** Complex          |\\\\n|                             | edits where the mistral family fits.                               | architecture or deep invariant reasoning.                           |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `codestral`                 | Low-latency code specialist: fill-in-the-middle, code correction,  | NOT dispatchable on this lane: the CLI parses `codestral` but the   |\\\\n|                             | test generation; 256K context per the provider's 25.01 model card  | API answers 400 invalid_model (probed 2026-11-03 and 11-04).        |\\\\n|                             | (source-derived, not probe-verified).                              | **Avoid for:** all routing until the lane revives it; narrow code   |\\\\n|                             |                                                                    | focus, not a reviewer.                                              |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `devstral`                  | The family's agentic software-engineering specialist (public       | NOT dispatchable on this lane: parses client-side, API 400          |\\\\n|                             | positioning; source-derived, not measured here). **Best for:**     | invalid_model (probed 2026-11-03 and 11-04). **Avoid for:** all     |\\\\n|                             | repo-level agentic coding sweeps if the lane ever revives it.      | routing today.                                                      |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `magistral-medium-latest`   | The family's reasoning-tuned medium line (source-derived, not      | NOT dispatchable via `vibe` at all: not in the CLI alias list       |\\\\n|                             | measured here). **Best for:** deliberative reasoning chains if a   | (Unknown model, probed 2026-11-04). **Avoid for:** all routing      |\\\\n|                             | lane ever wires it.                                                | today.                                                              |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `mixtral-8x7b`              | Sparse Mixture of Experts; strong coding at lower cost.            | **Avoid for:** Tasks requiring dense attention across very long     |\\\\n|                             | **Best for:** Implementation, debugging, and moderate complexity.   | contexts or fine-grained invariant verification.                   |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `muse-wonder-1.0`           | Flagship Muse (Meta) model; the model behind the Muse              | Heavier and slower than Spark. **Avoid for:** High-volume trivial   |\\\\n|                             | orchestrator seat in this repo: long-horizon agentic coding,       | edits, extraction, or latency-sensitive loops; use                  |\\\\n|                             | multi-file implementation, and deep cross-checking.                | `muse-spark-1.3` instead.                                           |\\\\n|                             | **Best for:** Orchestration, complex implementation, deep review.  |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `muse-spark-1.3`            | Fast Muse lane; the auxiliary/helper model inside Muse             | Lighter reasoning than Wonder. **Avoid for:** Hardest architecture, |\\\\n|                             | sessions. **Best for:** Scoped subagent work, bounded searches,    | security sign-offs, or subtle invariant proofs; escalate to         |\\\\n|                             | routine QA, extraction, and fast iteration.                        | `muse-wonder-1.0`.                                                  |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `qwen3.8-max`               | Qwen (Alibaba) flagship on the `qwen` CLI's Token Plan (serving    | Capability not benchmarked from this fleet yet (availability        |\\\\n|                             | model, probed live 2026-11-04); latest generation. **Best for:**   | measured only); treat early dispatches as candidates for the        |\\\\n|                             | Implementation and review rounds on the qwen lane, pending in-repo | standing kimi quality gate before trusting verdicts. **Avoid for:** |\\\\n|                             | evaluation.                                                        | Long-context jobs: context size not probe-verified.                 |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `qwen3.8-flash`             | Qwen fast tier on the same plan (probed live 2026-11-04). **Best   | Lighter tier than 3.8-max. **Avoid for:** Hardest reasoning or      |\\\\n|                             | for:** Latency-sensitive iteration, QA spot-checks, extraction,    | disputed findings; use `qwen3.8-max` or escalate per the ladder.    |\\\\n|                             | and this seat's own interactive runs.                              |                                                                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `qwen3.7-max`               | Prior-generation Qwen flagship, still served on the plan (probed   | Superseded within the lane by `qwen3.8-max`. **Avoid for:** Any     |\\\\n|                             | live). Second choice when 3.8 capacity is throttled.               | work 3.8 can take; no measured advantage over it.                   |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `qwen3.6-flash`             | Prior-generation Qwen fast tier (probed live).                     | Two generations old; lighter reasoning than either 3.8 tier.        |\\\\n|                             |                                                                    | **Avoid for:** Anything the 3.8 lane can serve.                     |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n| `deepseek-v4-pro` (qwen     | NOTE: the `qwen` lane's Token Plan ALSO serves `deepseek-v4-pro`   | Same-family collision: a deepseek-v4-pro author on the qwen lane    |\\\\n| lane note)                  | (probed live 2026-11-04), in addition to the Qwen tiers.           | must NOT be reviewed by the DeepSeek lane (same family).            |\\\\n+-----------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------+\\\\n## Role Selection & Reviewer Independence\\\\n\\\\nThe Orchestrator is completely responsible for selecting the implementer for a given role. It dynamically\\\\ncross-references the task's requirements against the **Model Strengths and Selection Guide** and the\\\\n**Available Subscriptions & Cost Constraints**. Any model may fill any role provided its technical profile and\\\\ncost align with the task.\\\\n\\\\n**Reviewer Independence Rules:**\\\\nEvery change must be reviewed by a model from a **different family** to ensure\\\\nindependent defect discovery. Never silently substitute a same-family reviewer.\\\\nThe families represented in the Model Strengths table are: **Claude**,\\\\n**DeepSeek**, **Gemini**, **GLM**, **GPT**, **Kimi**, **Mistral**,\\\\n**Muse** (Meta), and **Qwen** (Alibaba). Family, not CLI, decides\\\\nindependence: the `qwen` lane's `deepseek-v4-pro` is a **DeepSeek**\\\\nauthor for review purposes, and Qwen-authored work (including anything\\\\nby this interactive seat) must be reviewed by a different family.\\\\nAny author from one family must be reviewed by a model from a different family.\\\\n\\\\nRoutine review applies only when independent; architectural or security review\\\\nis mandatory when the role or risk requires it. For operational procedures, see\\\\nthe [`Lead Engineering Orchestrator`](./roles/orchestrator.md).\\\\n\\\\n**Reviewer bench and parallel-lane scheduling** (2026-10-26 owner ruling,\\\\nafter the jolt lane's throughput review):\\\\n\\\\n- **The standing reviewer bench** is wider than the implementation bench:\\\\n  `kimi` (flat membership, native Kimi models only) joins the families above\\\\n  as a standing review destination. The independence rule is unchanged: the\\\\n  reviewer must be a different model family from the author.\\\\n- **Parallel-lane scheduling**: when slice N lands on its branch, slice N+1's\\\\n  implementation dispatches immediately and slice N's review runs\\\\n  concurrently. Gate: review N must complete **before slice N+1 merges** — a\\\\n  defect found in N's review must still be fixable without N+1 having built\\\\n  on it. Judgment is required on what N+1 actually built on; tightly-coupled\\\\n  slices serialize.\\\\n- **Shared ledgers scale with concurrency**: the pre-push row-count check on\\\\n  append-only ledgers (`docs/agents/known-gaps.md`; orchestrator.md Workflow\\\\n  step 10) is more important under parallel review, not less — parallel\\\\n  review means parallel merges (the 2026-10-25 corruption precedent:\\\\n  merge 4d6fd818 silently dropped ~66 ledger rows by replacing the file).\\\\n- Never relax the independence rule or skip reviews on small slices; the fix\\\\n  for throughput is more reviewers, not fewer reviews.\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.started\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg --files -g '*1794600000000*' -g '*attribution*' -g '*contracts*' -g '*ADDENDUM*'; rg -n '8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.1|address-linked adaptation|durable correspondence|caller-site' docs orchestrator-logs | head -100\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":null,\\\"status\\\":\\\"in_progress\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg --files -g '*1794600000000*' -g '*attribution*' -g '*contracts*' -g '*ADDENDUM*'; rg -n '8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.1|address-linked adaptation|durable correspondence|caller-site' docs orchestrator-logs | head -100\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.glm-5.3.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling-of-record.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a-DRAFT.md\\\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-engineer-attribution-as-query.prompt.md\\\\ncollab/yang-python/1793410000000-orchestrator-yang-python-attribution-note.md\\\\ncollab/orch-lane/1794600000000-engineer-roundA-p1-fixes.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\\\\ncollab/orch-lane/1794700000001-engineer-roundB1-p9-contracts.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.stdout.log\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:32:## 2026-10-30 00:05 +07 — the official handoff brief arrives: the seat's assignment is the 8.10 common-call adoption\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:36:  (yang.antlr.md 8.10), coordinating the shared runtime dependency with the TypeScript/PHP\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:46:Next: read 8.10 + the kimi R2 approval; verify the codex checkout; read the two sibling\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:103:  committed 8.10.3 keys (:yang.call/callee, :yang.call/positional, :yang.call/named, :yang.call/raise;\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:134:## 2026-10-30 10:0x +07 — S1 LANDED to master (10a24952): the first 8.10 slice, end to end\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:217:  READY (session_664f487a) — \\\\\\\"implements 8.10.4 without introducing language-pair dispatch\\\\\\\", the\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:226:  from any conforming frontend's source through one mechanism — the 8.10 promise, integrated.\\\\norchestrator-logs/1793292851-yang-python-retake.md:227:Next: the 8.10.11 conformance evidence (bidirectional/higher-order cross-language fixtures,\\\\norchestrator-logs/